00:01:17.980 --> 00:01:24.640
In the spring of 1805, the Lewis and Clark expedition reached what is now Montana, near

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:30.000
where the Yellowstone and Missouri rivers meet, moving farther west than any white American

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:31.280
Americans had ever gone.

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:38.270
Along the way, they had encountered tribes of native people who for hundreds of generations

00:01:38.270 --> 00:01:41.370
had called the bountiful land home.

00:01:43.310 --> 00:01:50.600
Wildlife seemed to be everywhere, and in astonishing numbers, Meriwether Lewis wrote, particularly

00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:51.830
the buffalo.

00:01:53.060 --> 00:02:00.000
The whole face of the country was covered with herds of buffalo, elk, and antelopes.

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:07.700
The buffalo frequently approach us, more nearly to discover what we are,

00:02:08.610 --> 00:02:14.820
and in some instances pursue us a considerable distance, apparently with that view.

00:02:17.450 --> 00:02:23.800
Less than a century later, in 1887, another expedition would explore the same region.

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:30.000
They hoped to find some buffaloes to kill and then preserve for an exhibit at the American Museum.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:32.560
Museum of Natural History in New York City.

00:02:32.920 --> 00:02:38.600
They searched for three months without seeing a single one.

00:02:40.050 --> 00:02:50.910
Everything the Kiowas had came from the buffalo.

00:02:52.050 --> 00:02:54.450
Their teepees were made of buffalo hide.

00:02:55.460 --> 00:02:57.720
So were their clothes and moccasins.

00:02:58.680 --> 00:03:00.000
They ate buffalo meat.

00:03:01.820 --> 00:03:06.780
Most of all, the buffalo was part of the Kiowa religion.

00:03:06.780 --> 00:03:10.360
The priests used parts of the buffalo to make their prayers

00:03:10.360 --> 00:03:15.000
when they healed people or when they sang to the powers above.

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:23.550
The buffalo were the life of the Kiowas, old lady horse.

00:03:23.550 --> 00:03:27.400
They are the national mammal of the United States,

00:03:27.400 --> 00:03:30.000
the largest land animals in the Western

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:35.130
A species that scientists call bison bison.

00:03:35.130 --> 00:03:43.060
Nourished by one of the world's greatest grasslands, they proliferated into herds of uncountable numbers.

00:03:43.060 --> 00:03:48.160
And in turn, by their grazing, nurtured the prairie that sustained them.

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:59.440
For more than 10,000 years, they evolved alongside indigenous people who relied on them for food and shelter.

00:04:00.830 --> 00:04:06.260
and in exchange for killing them, revered them.

00:04:06.260 --> 00:04:11.740
So much of my blood memory has to do with Buffalo.

00:04:11.740 --> 00:04:15.250
We have regard for each other, and we are friends,

00:04:15.250 --> 00:04:18.120
we are brothers, we are related.

00:04:18.120 --> 00:04:23.030
So I, you know, think of them in a particular way,

00:04:23.030 --> 00:04:26.770
and it's always with reverence.

00:04:26.770 --> 00:04:30.000
Newcomers to the continent found them fascinating at first.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:37.270
first, but in time came to consider them a hindrance, and then a source of profit for

00:04:37.270 --> 00:04:40.980
a growing nation.

00:04:40.980 --> 00:04:46.780
In the space of only a decade, they were slaughtered by the millions for their hides, with their

00:04:46.780 --> 00:04:54.100
carcasses left to rot on the prairies, the species itself teetering on the brink of disappearing

00:04:54.100 --> 00:04:57.010
forever from the face of the earth.

00:05:01.240 --> 00:05:07.420
The story of American bison really is two different stories.

00:05:07.420 --> 00:05:10.260
It really is a story of indigenous people

00:05:10.260 --> 00:05:14.780
and their relationship with the bison for thousands of years.

00:05:14.780 --> 00:05:20.570
And then enter, not just the Europeans, but the Americans.

00:05:20.570 --> 00:05:23.460
And that's a completely different story.

00:05:23.460 --> 00:05:28.440
And that really is a story of utter destruction.

00:05:28.440 --> 00:05:30.000
- It's not just the story of

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:38.960
this magnificent animal, it takes us into all the different corners of our history and

00:05:38.960 --> 00:05:44.600
how we interact with one another as human beings.

00:05:44.600 --> 00:05:51.970
It is a heartbreaking story of a collision of two different views of how human beings

00:05:51.970 --> 00:05:55.290
should interact with the natural world.

00:05:55.290 --> 00:05:58.770
And there's a tragedy at the very heart of that story.

00:06:01.180 --> 00:06:07.160
At the same time, as you follow it a little bit farther down that trail, it can offer us hope.

00:06:45.130 --> 00:06:51.350
these big slightly strange looking but magnificent magnificent animals and they're ours

00:06:51.880 --> 00:07:00.000
right they're our animal if you see one out grazing it looks so slow it's like a

00:07:00.030 --> 00:07:06.440
parked car sitting there but they can clear six-foot fences they can jump a

00:07:06.440 --> 00:07:13.810
horizontal jump of seven feet they can hit a speed hit the speed of 35 miles an

00:07:13.810 --> 00:07:17.450
hour and you're talking about something that can get going that speed that's

00:07:17.450 --> 00:07:21.940
1800 pounds it's like a souped-up hot rod of an

00:07:21.940 --> 00:07:30.000
animal hiding in a minivan shell fully grown an American Buffalo can wave

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:36.110
more than a ton, stand taller than six feet at the shoulder, and stretch more

00:07:36.110 --> 00:07:42.960
than ten feet long, not including the tail. Huge as they are, they are small

00:07:42.960 --> 00:07:47.300
compared to some of the prehistoric animals that once roamed the continent.

00:07:47.300 --> 00:07:54.600
Woolly mammoths, giant ground sloths and camels, and other species of bison, one

