of which had horns that spanned nine feet from tip to tip.

00:08:00.960 --> 00:08:05.790
after humans arrived in north america more than 20 000 years ago

00:08:05.790 --> 00:08:10.390
all of the biggest animals along with nearly 50 other species

00:08:10.390 --> 00:08:15.180
went extinct on the continent from either hunting or changing climate

00:08:15.180 --> 00:08:19.780
or a combination of the two in their place

00:08:19.780 --> 00:08:23.440
the modern buffalo evolved and multiplied

00:08:23.440 --> 00:08:30.000
particularly on the grasslands of the great plains bison and humans in

00:08:30.090 --> 00:08:34.430
in a real sense, co-evolved alongside one another

00:08:34.430 --> 00:08:37.310
over the last 10,000 years or so.

00:08:37.310 --> 00:08:40.560
Sometimes the animals would ebb and flow,

00:08:40.560 --> 00:08:44.030
but they always rebounded.

00:08:44.030 --> 00:08:48.510
And so there was this wonderful kind of dynamic equilibrium

00:08:48.510 --> 00:08:52.730
that lasted for more than 10,000 years.

00:08:52.730 --> 00:08:56.080
- They have always lived with humans.

00:08:56.080 --> 00:09:00.000
They've always been hunted by humans, they've always had

00:09:00.060 --> 00:09:09.300
predators. So their entire sort of evolution as an animal species has been as an animal

00:09:09.300 --> 00:09:11.840
that has been hunted.

00:09:11.840 --> 00:09:18.590
And their primary defense mechanism is to run away. And they have that skill at a very

00:09:18.590 --> 00:09:23.340
young age. A newborn buffalo calf tries to stand

00:09:23.340 --> 00:09:30.000
for the first time at the age of two minutes. And at seven minutes, they're able to run

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:35.080
done with the herd.

00:09:35.080 --> 00:09:40.630
Over the centuries, their grazing habits on the wide expanses of the Great Plains proved

00:09:40.630 --> 00:09:46.600
crucial to its ecology, the types of grasses that flourished there, and the other species

00:09:46.600 --> 00:09:53.520
that thrived alongside the buffalo. Even when they stopped and sometimes dug through the

00:09:53.520 --> 00:10:00.000
grass with their horns, and then rolled in the dust, creating buffalo wallows, the bison

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:04.220
Bison's habits helped support other forms of life on the plains.

00:10:04.220 --> 00:10:07.710
It's not just one wallow.

00:10:07.710 --> 00:10:13.880
We're talking about millions of bison, which means millions of wallows.

00:10:13.880 --> 00:10:16.660
Those wallows could do a couple of things.

00:10:16.660 --> 00:10:20.530
At its most simple and basic, it's a dirt bath.

00:10:20.530 --> 00:10:24.160
But then it also has an ecosystem function, water retention.

00:10:24.160 --> 00:10:30.000
If it rained, these become shallow little ponds and pools, and that in turn affects

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:33.720
affected the landscape as well.

00:10:33.720 --> 00:10:36.350
Because it's also a disturbed area,

00:10:36.350 --> 00:10:39.850
plants that flourish in disturbed areas

00:10:39.850 --> 00:10:44.380
will also then grow around a wallow.

00:10:44.380 --> 00:10:47.120
So they became these really great areas,

00:10:47.120 --> 00:10:49.800
not only for wildlife to use,

00:10:49.800 --> 00:10:51.960
but also for humans to use

00:10:51.960 --> 00:10:55.810
because of the plants that were there.

00:10:55.810 --> 00:10:59.030
When the buffalo are here, the land is good.

00:10:59.030 --> 00:11:00.000
When the land is good,

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:07.500
lower healthy. We have lived here for 600 generations. We have been here conservatively

00:11:07.500 --> 00:11:15.560
12,000 years. So if you think about that 12,000 years, imagine that on a timeline and then

00:11:15.560 --> 00:11:21.840
take that 12,000 years and wrap that timeline around a 24-hour clock. What that means is

00:11:21.840 --> 00:11:30.000
that Columbus arrived at about 11.28 p.m. and

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:35.480
and Lewis and Clark at about 15 minutes before midnight.

00:11:35.500 --> 00:11:38.540
Native Americans seamlessly wove the animals

00:11:38.580 --> 00:11:43.740
into every aspect of their daily lives and religious beliefs.

00:11:43.760 --> 00:11:45.940
The buffalo was iconic and sacred

00:11:45.960 --> 00:11:50.210
and became so deeply ingrained in the life of the tribe

00:11:50.250 --> 00:11:55.120
that they could not imagine existence without the buffalo.

00:11:55.160 --> 00:11:58.580
In the ancient origin stories of many tribes,

00:11:58.600 --> 00:12:00.000
the bison were among the earliest

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:07.090
earliest animals created, often emerging before human beings from underground in what became

00:12:07.090 --> 00:12:14.540
sacred sites, like Wind Cave in the Black Hills of what is now South Dakota or Oklahoma's

00:12:14.540 --> 00:12:21.390
Wichita Mountains, whose most prominent peak is now called Mount Scott.

00:12:21.390 --> 00:12:28.680
The Kiowas in particular believed that this was the mountain from which buffalo had originally

00:12:28.680 --> 00:12:29.640
emerged.

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:36.760
And that whenever they went away, and buffalo did go away in the remembered histories of

00:12:36.760 --> 00:12:42.630
tribal people, this is where, on the southern plains, the buffalo went.

00:12:42.630 --> 00:12:48.580
The Cheyenne and Lakota each have their own stories about a contest between people and

00:12:48.580 --> 00:12:53.830
bison to determine which one would have mastery over the other.

00:12:53.830 --> 00:13:00.000
In a long and arduous race circling the Black Hills, some of the animals died and stained

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:10.700
soil red forever with their blood in the end the people won the old buffalo bulls called the young

00:13:10.700 --> 00:13:19.210
man to come to them well you have won they said you are on top now all we animals can do is supply

00:13:19.210 --> 00:13:30.000
the things that you will use from us our meat and skins and bones and we will teach you the sundance john

00:13:30.060 --> 00:13:37.400
stands in timber. Every tribe on the plains held ceremonies related to the

00:13:37.400 --> 00:13:43.600
buffalo, who it was said had their own families and clans, their own societies

00:13:43.600 --> 00:13:50.490
and customs, and were capable of changing forms to communicate directly with humans.

00:13:50.490 --> 00:14:00.000
The Mandan in what is now North Dakota had the White Buffalo Cow Society, respected older women whose

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:04.890
The leader wrapped herself in the robe of a rare and sacred white buffalo

00:14:04.890 --> 00:14:10.630
as they danced all night to call the bison herds closer.

00:14:10.630 --> 00:14:17.000
In a different ceremony, experienced hunters costumed themselves as buffalo bulls,

00:14:17.000 --> 00:14:23.310
whose power, called medicine, could be shared with others in the tribe.

00:14:23.310 --> 00:14:27.450
The first thing I was told about buffalo was not really the hunting part of it.

00:14:27.450 --> 00:14:30.000
First thing I was told about them was the spirituality.

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:35.040
part of it, about how they were created by our creator, how they were put on this earth

00:14:35.040 --> 00:14:42.550
to help us survive, not only with clothing, with warmth, with food, with tools, but with

00:14:42.550 --> 00:14:46.910
the essential, which was the spirit of the buffalo, and how the spirit was part of us

00:14:46.910 --> 00:14:50.500
and we were part of them.

00:14:50.500 --> 00:14:57.010
Each summer, the Lakota, like many tribes, gathered for a sun dance, their most important

00:14:57.010 --> 00:15:00.000
ceremony, which renewed their relationship with

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:05.190
wakantonka, the great spirit of the universe that permeates everything.

00:15:05.190 --> 00:15:12.470
Buffaloes were considered the animal with the most direct connection to that life force.

00:15:12.470 --> 00:15:18.620
Over the course of many generations, the Kiowa had moved from the mountains near the headwaters

00:15:18.620 --> 00:15:25.160
of the Yellowstone River, down to the Northern Plains, then to the Black Hills, and eventually

00:15:25.160 --> 00:15:29.640
farther south to the Wichita Mountains in what is now Oklahoma.

00:15:30.800 --> 00:15:37.040
Along the way, they learned their Sundance from the crows.

00:15:37.040 --> 00:15:41.300
The Sundance was an indispensable part of the Kiowa life,

00:15:41.300 --> 00:15:45.940
and the buffalo was the sacrificial victim of the Sundance.

00:15:45.940 --> 00:15:49.060
Could not have a Sundance without killing a buffalo bull

00:15:49.060 --> 00:15:52.760
