00:23:24.610 --> 00:23:26.970
that Europeans had not known existed.

00:23:28.360 --> 00:23:30.000
Nothing would ever be the same

00:23:30.030 --> 00:23:35.990
for people on either side of the Atlantic Ocean. For the indigenous

00:23:35.990 --> 00:23:42.390
populations of the Americas, it would prove catastrophic. In some tribes, nearly

00:23:42.390 --> 00:23:49.640
90% would perish from diseases for which they had little immunity. Wave after wave

00:23:49.640 --> 00:23:54.790
of epidemics swept across the hemisphere as European powers competed

00:23:54.790 --> 00:24:00.000
to exploit the bountiful resources and countless natural wonders that the

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:04.200
continent seemed to offer for the taking.

00:24:04.200 --> 00:24:09.360
One of the most fascinating wonders was the bison.

00:24:09.360 --> 00:24:13.550
Wandering across what is now Texas in the 1530s,

00:24:13.550 --> 00:24:17.910
Alvar Nunez Cabeza de Vaca and three other survivors

00:24:17.910 --> 00:24:21.390
of a Spanish shipwreck became the first Europeans

00:24:21.390 --> 00:24:25.330
to encounter American buffalo when a tribe they met

00:24:25.330 --> 00:24:28.770
fed the starving strangers with the animal's meat.

00:24:30.120 --> 00:24:35.980
Less than a decade later, a conquistador named Francisco Vasquez de Coronado

00:24:35.980 --> 00:24:39.920
led his mounted soldiers onto the Great Plains,

00:24:39.920 --> 00:24:43.920
pursuing rumors of cities filled with silver and gold.

00:24:43.920 --> 00:24:48.270
Instead, he found villages of Wichita Indians.

00:24:48.270 --> 00:24:52.840
But he and his men were astonished by the landscape

00:24:52.840 --> 00:24:55.840
and the huge herds of buffalo roaming across it.

00:24:55.840 --> 00:25:00.000
There was not a day I lost sight of them,

00:25:00.000 --> 00:25:05.020
amazed Coronado wrote. The only way to describe their numbers was to compare

00:25:05.020 --> 00:25:12.280
them to the fishes of the sea. His army killed and ate 500 bison on their

00:25:12.280 --> 00:25:17.930
futile quest for gold and stacked piles of dung to mark their route for the

00:25:17.930 --> 00:25:24.230
return trip. They then wrote the king of Spain that a fortune could be made in

00:25:24.230 --> 00:25:30.000
turning buffalo hides into leather. They saw this animal in

00:25:30.120 --> 00:25:36.220
incredible profusion. And they thought, "God, you know, what's in it for us? How can we

00:25:36.220 --> 00:25:39.770
profit from this?"

00:25:39.770 --> 00:25:45.790
Buffalo were nearly everywhere in North America. No one knows exactly how many bison once

00:25:45.790 --> 00:25:52.570
existed on the continent, but it was in the many tens of millions. Their range extended

00:25:52.570 --> 00:25:59.220
from west of the Rocky Mountains into Idaho, Oregon, and Washington, from northern Mexico

00:25:59.220 --> 00:26:00.000
into Canada.

00:26:00.000 --> 00:26:10.700
from Florida to Lake Erie. In 1613, sailing up the Potomac River, one of the

00:26:10.700 --> 00:26:16.480
early Jamestown colonists came across a herd near what is now Washington DC.

00:26:16.480 --> 00:26:23.080
But as English colonies grew along the Atlantic coast, the number of buffalo

00:26:23.080 --> 00:26:30.000
east of the Appalachian Mountains dwindled. Worried that the animals were disappearing,

00:26:30.220 --> 00:26:37.160
In 1759, Georgia's provincial legislature made it illegal to hunt them in some parts

00:26:37.160 --> 00:26:39.150
of the colony.

00:26:39.150 --> 00:26:42.230
No one enforced the law.

00:26:42.230 --> 00:26:48.470
When Daniel Boone opened the wilderness trail into Kentucky for settlers eager for new lands,

00:26:48.470 --> 00:26:53.520
the route he followed through the Cumberland Gap was called a buffalo trace, which the

00:26:53.520 --> 00:26:57.220
animals had been using for centuries.

00:26:57.220 --> 00:27:00.000
West of the mountains, he wrote, the buffaloes were

00:27:00.000 --> 00:27:04.880
more frequent than I have seen cattle in the settlements.

00:27:04.880 --> 00:27:07.230
As a young surveyor and soldier,

00:27:07.230 --> 00:27:09.830
George Washington had once hunted buffalo

00:27:09.830 --> 00:27:12.830
near the Ohio River.

00:27:12.830 --> 00:27:16.350
In 1775, just before he left

00:27:16.350 --> 00:27:18.990
for the Second Continental Congress,

00:27:18.990 --> 00:27:21.900
he hired a man to capture some calves

00:27:21.900 --> 00:27:25.350
so he could raise them on his Mount Vernon plantation.

00:27:30.640 --> 00:27:36.370
By the early 1800s, nearly all the bison east of the Mississippi were gone.

00:27:36.370 --> 00:27:42.600
But in the Great Plains, an estimated 30 million buffalo still roamed,

00:27:42.600 --> 00:27:46.960
along with 120,000 native people.

00:27:46.960 --> 00:27:51.120
Life there, for the Indians and the buffalo,

00:27:51.120 --> 00:27:57.140
had already been transformed by something else the Europeans had brought to North America.

00:27:57.140 --> 00:27:59.600
Years before,

00:28:00.000 --> 00:28:04.930
Cheyenne Prophet's sweet medicine had told his people about it, too.

00:28:06.280 --> 00:28:09.070
There will be an animal you must learn to use.

00:28:10.440 --> 00:28:13.740
It has a shaggy neck and a tail almost touching the ground.

00:28:14.790 --> 00:28:15.630
Its hooves are round.

00:28:17.950 --> 00:28:21.510
This animal will carry you on his back and help you in many ways.

00:28:21.690 --> 00:28:29.250
Those far hills that seem only a blue vision in the distance take many days to reach now.

00:28:30.030 --> 00:28:32.690
But with this animal, you can get there in a short time.

00:28:33.380 --> 00:28:34.240
So fear it not.

00:28:35.570 --> 00:28:36.590
Remember what I have said.

00:28:38.800 --> 00:28:42.540
Spanish conquistadors had used horses to great effect

00:28:42.540 --> 00:28:47.170
in battles with native people who had never seen such animals before.

00:28:47.170 --> 00:28:52.730
But in 1680, the Pueblo tribes had risen up in revolt

00:28:52.730 --> 00:28:55.570
and drove the Spanish out of New Mexico.

00:28:57.150 --> 00:29:00.000
Their horse herds remained and flourished.

00:29:01.950 --> 00:29:10.170
In less than a century, the horse had spread from one tribe to another throughout the West.

00:29:10.170 --> 00:29:14.070
The coming of the horse brought about such a revolution.

00:29:14.070 --> 00:29:25.290
Suddenly, it was magic and indispensable and changed their lives completely.

00:29:25.290 --> 00:29:30.000
A mounted hunter could now kill enough buffalo in one day to feed

00:29:30.000 --> 00:29:33.090
and clothe his family for months.

00:29:33.090 --> 00:29:36.450
And with horses, not dogs, pulling a teepee

00:29:36.450 --> 00:29:40.280
and belongings on a travoi, families could travel farther

00:29:40.280 --> 00:29:44.580
into the vastness of the plains to pursue the herds.

00:29:44.580 --> 00:29:47.080
Some tribes left their permanent villages

00:29:47.080 --> 00:29:49.830
and cultivated fields altogether

00:29:49.830 --> 00:29:52.630
to become semi-nomadic hunters

00:29:52.630 --> 00:29:57.920
and among the greatest equestrians in the world.

00:29:57.920 --> 00:30:00.000
- Between 1730 and 1830,

00:30:00.000 --> 00:30:07.940
As many as three dozen different tribes living either on the margins of the plains or in

00:30:07.940 --> 00:30:13.860
some cases even farther distant than that, mounted up on horses, abandoned their farming

00:30:13.860 --> 00:30:16.580
plots and rode out of the plains to hunt buffalo.

00:30:17.550 --> 00:30:24.950
So that combination of those two great Pleistocene animals, the horse and the bison, became a

00:30:24.950 --> 00:30:30.000
revolution in American history that produced the classic Plains

00:30:30.000 --> 00:30:37.650
Indian buffalo hunter. The great herds of buffalo were constantly moving. And of course,

00:30:37.650 --> 00:30:44.970
we moved when they did. All that was changed by the horse. Even the old people could ride.

00:30:46.400 --> 00:30:56.420
Ah, I came into a happy world. There was always fat meat, glad singing, and much dancing in our

00:30:56.420 --> 00:31:00.000
villages. Our people's hearts were...

00:31:00.060 --> 00:31:10.560
than as light as breath feathers. Pretty shield. If you think of a human being on

00:31:10.560 --> 00:31:16.190
the back of a horse as a kind of a new species, a single animal, a single animal,

00:31:16.190 --> 00:31:23.680
a horse man hyphen, not a horseman, but a horse man. This is an animal that has the

00:31:23.680 --> 00:31:30.000
strength and the power of a horse drawn from the sunlight in those grasses and the grace of a

00:31:30.000 --> 00:31:34.620
but it has the intelligence and the imagination

00:31:34.620 --> 00:31:38.850
and the ambition and the arrogance of a human being.

00:31:38.850 --> 00:31:40.310
That's a new animal.

00:31:40.310 --> 00:31:43.610
This was something that the bison had never faced before,

00:31:43.610 --> 00:31:46.040
and that was trouble.

00:31:46.040 --> 00:31:50.490
Some 30 tribes converged on the Great Plains

00:31:50.490 --> 00:31:52.490
from every direction,

00:31:52.490 --> 00:31:55.790
each of them increasingly dependent on the bison

00:31:55.790 --> 00:31:58.790
for their sustenance and prosperity,

00:31:58.790 --> 00:32:00.000
and equal to their success.

00:32:00.000 --> 00:32:04.800
dependent on the horse for their hunting and their defense against their

00:32:04.800 --> 00:32:14.590
enemies meanwhile European powers the Spanish French Russians and British were

00:32:14.590 --> 00:32:22.190
locked in their own contest over the destiny of the American West this immense

00:32:22.190 --> 00:32:27.650
river waters one of the fairest portions of the globe nor do I believe

00:32:27.650 --> 00:32:30.000
that there is in the universe a similar

00:32:30.000 --> 00:32:33.130
extent of country.

00:32:33.130 --> 00:32:39.750
As we passed on, it seemed as if those scenes of visionary enchantment would never have

00:32:39.750 --> 00:32:41.820
an end.

00:32:41.820 --> 00:32:42.820
Meriwether Lewis.

00:32:42.820 --> 00:32:50.500
In 1803, the young United States joined the competition.

00:32:50.500 --> 00:32:56.570
President Thomas Jefferson's Louisiana Purchase extended his nation's western boundary from

00:32:56.570 --> 00:32:59.600
the Mississippi all the way to the Rocky Mountains.

00:33:01.210 --> 00:33:02.870
- Jefferson then dispatched

00:33:02.870 --> 00:33:06.440
the Lewis and Clark expedition up the Missouri River

00:33:06.440 --> 00:33:11.070
to study the land's terrain and potential.

00:33:11.070 --> 00:33:14.470
- They began to see a whole host of creatures

00:33:14.470 --> 00:33:17.260
that none of these people had ever seen.

00:33:17.260 --> 00:33:21.100
They see their first mule deer, their first magpies,

00:33:21.100 --> 00:33:24.640
their first coyotes, their first prairie dogs,

00:33:24.640 --> 00:33:26.620
their first pronghorns.

00:33:26.620 --> 00:33:30.000
One animal after another that no one had any inkling.

00:33:30.000 --> 00:33:32.300
actually existed in North America

00:33:32.300 --> 00:33:34.500
is suddenly showing up in front of them.

00:33:35.940 --> 00:33:38.220
And one of the things they began seeing, of course,

00:33:38.360 --> 00:33:41.980
are increasingly larger and larger herds of bison

00:33:41.980 --> 00:33:47.000
stretching to those unfathomable distances across the horizon.

00:33:48.080 --> 00:33:51.660
So what they preserved for us in their journals

00:33:51.660 --> 00:33:54.840
is a glimpse into the America

00:33:54.840 --> 00:33:59.000
that had existed for 10,000 years before us.

00:34:00.960 --> 00:34:03.940
On their return trip from the Pacific Ocean,

00:34:03.940 --> 00:34:07.120
the explorers had to halt their dugout canoes

00:34:07.120 --> 00:34:09.840
for more than an hour on the Yellowstone

00:34:09.840 --> 00:34:14.000
as a herd swam across the river in front of them.

00:34:14.000 --> 00:34:16.900
- And Clark says as they're coming down the Yellowstone,

00:34:16.900 --> 00:34:20.630
I saw more buffalo than I've ever seen before today.

00:34:20.630 --> 00:34:23.960
And he tries to give some sense of the numbers.

00:34:23.960 --> 00:34:25.000
Then a few days later he says,

00:34:25.000 --> 00:34:27.340
well now today I saw more buffalo

00:34:27.340 --> 00:34:29.160
than I've ever seen before.

00:34:30.000 --> 00:34:33.080
And finally he says, "I'm not going to write about it anymore," because no one would believe

00:34:33.080 --> 00:34:38.940
it, that the numbers are essentially infinite.

00:34:38.940 --> 00:34:45.350
These strangers will be a people who do not get tired, but who will keep pushing forward,

00:34:45.350 --> 00:34:48.430
going and going all the time.

00:34:48.430 --> 00:34:54.810
They will keep coming, coming, following nothing that they do, but keep your own ways that

00:34:54.810 --> 00:35:00.000
I have taught you as long as you can, sweet medicine.

00:35:02.490 --> 00:35:09.110
Native people had been exchanging animal pelts for European trade goods for more than two centuries.

00:35:09.110 --> 00:35:15.150
When Lewis and Clark returned with reports of rivers teeming with beaver,

00:35:15.150 --> 00:35:23.600
fur companies responded by sending squadrons of trappers, called mountain men, into every corner of the West.

00:35:23.600 --> 00:35:30.000
All to feed the demand in New York, Paris and London for fashionable hats made of beaver.

00:35:30.000 --> 00:35:37.460
And this was really the first step in which Indian peoples of the far west began to become

00:35:37.460 --> 00:35:44.280
enmeshed and caught up in this global economy, and so it made them vulnerable in ways that

00:35:44.280 --> 00:35:47.570
they could not possibly have anticipated.

00:35:47.570 --> 00:35:54.830
By the 1830s, the mountain man era had ended. The fashion had changed to silk hats, and

00:35:54.830 --> 00:36:00.000
most of the beaver had been trapped out. But there were still tens of millions of people

00:36:00.000 --> 00:36:02.750
of buffalo.

00:36:02.750 --> 00:36:07.650
Consumers in the East had now developed a taste for salted buffalo tongues.

00:36:07.650 --> 00:36:14.360
Thick buffalo robes became popular to keep people warm while riding in their carriages.

00:36:14.360 --> 00:36:20.070
Along the Missouri River and its tributaries, the fur companies established dozens of trading

00:36:20.070 --> 00:36:26.800
posts, where tribes bartered buffalo robes and tongues for goods manufactured in Europe

00:36:26.800 --> 00:36:30.000
and the East, metal pots to make their lives

00:36:30.090 --> 00:36:38.020
easier, colorful glass beads and woven blankets, and guns for hunting or fighting their enemies.

00:36:39.690 --> 00:36:46.110
Preparing a buffalo robe for market took time and hard work, from painstakingly scraping

00:36:46.110 --> 00:36:51.770
away the flesh and fat to softening the hide by patiently rubbing it with cooked bison

00:36:51.770 --> 00:36:52.310
brains.

00:36:52.310 --> 00:36:59.090
The semi-nomadic tribes were already killing more buffalo than they needed for their own

00:36:59.090 --> 00:37:00.000
subsistence.

00:37:00.410 --> 00:37:06.880
in order to trade with agricultural tribes for corn and squash and tobacco.

00:37:06.880 --> 00:37:13.460
Now, they killed even more to meet the demand of white people far away.

00:37:13.460 --> 00:37:17.580
When large steamboats replaced smaller keelboats and canoes,

00:37:17.580 --> 00:37:22.340
the volume of trade exploded even further.

00:37:22.340 --> 00:37:26.530
The first steamboat to ply the Missouri returned to St. Louis,

00:37:26.530 --> 00:37:30.000
loaded down with stacks of buffalo robes and

00:37:30.000 --> 00:37:33.350
10,000 pounds of tongues.

00:37:33.350 --> 00:37:48.220
In one five-year period, New Orleans handled more than 750,000 robes bound for the East.

00:37:48.220 --> 00:37:54.850
Many Plains tribes kept a pictorial calendar of each passing year, a painted image, often

00:37:54.850 --> 00:37:59.670
on a buffalo hide, depicting the event they remembered most vividly.

00:38:00.090 --> 00:38:06.420
For some, it might be a battle with their enemies, a successful hunt, or the outbreak of a disease.

00:38:07.020 --> 00:38:10.580
But one year, they all recorded the same thing.

00:38:11.390 --> 00:38:14.910
They remembered it as the year the stars fell.

00:38:14.910 --> 00:38:23.010
On November 13, 1833, the largest meteor shower ever witnessed,

00:38:23.640 --> 00:38:30.000
an estimated 72,000 shooting stars per hour, burst over much of North America.

00:38:30.990 --> 00:38:36.350
Townspeople on the east coast were mesmerized by the display.

00:38:36.350 --> 00:38:43.700
For people living in teepees on the open prairie, the spectacle was overwhelming.

00:38:43.700 --> 00:38:47.090
The Kiowas were camped in the Wichita mountains.

00:38:47.090 --> 00:38:49.700
The stars went crazy in the sky.

00:38:49.700 --> 00:38:52.830
It seemed that the world was coming to an end.

00:38:52.830 --> 00:39:00.000
They were awakened by the light of flashing stars, and they ran out into, into the,

00:39:00.800 --> 00:39:05.360
out into the false day and were terrified.

00:39:05.360 --> 00:39:09.960
They think the year and the event is being an omen.

00:39:09.960 --> 00:39:14.380
Bad things came after that.

00:39:14.380 --> 00:39:19.400
The United States was pushing westward.

00:39:19.400 --> 00:39:24.160
Within 15 years, its boundary would stretch to the Pacific.

00:39:24.160 --> 00:39:26.970
To get there, all of the overland trails

00:39:26.970 --> 00:39:30.000
had to cross the Great Plains, still controlled

00:39:30.090 --> 00:39:36.370
by the native tribes who lived there Americans had different motives for their

00:39:36.370 --> 00:39:42.030
migrations but the huge bison herds they encountered played a role in

00:39:42.030 --> 00:39:50.620
everyone's journey I never saw anything like buffalo meat to satisfy hunger so

00:39:50.620 --> 00:40:00.000
long as there is buffalo meat I do not wish anything else Narcissa Whitman in

00:40:00.060 --> 00:40:04.060
Narcissa Whitman was headed to the Pacific Northwest

00:40:04.060 --> 00:40:07.980
to help her missionary husband convert indigenous people

00:40:07.980 --> 00:40:12.890
to Christianity. Other Americans were heading to Oregon

00:40:12.890 --> 00:40:16.970
to establish farms, to California to pan for gold,

00:40:16.970 --> 00:40:22.130
and to Santa Fe for commerce. The Mormons went to Utah

00:40:22.130 --> 00:40:26.330
to find refuge from religious persecution. On the way,

00:40:26.330 --> 00:40:30.000
their leaders used bleached buffalo skulls as

00:40:30.000 --> 00:40:39.440
signposts, leaving instructions to those following behind indicating prime camping places.

00:40:39.440 --> 00:40:42.850
Aristocrats from Europe were also showing up.

00:40:42.850 --> 00:40:48.740
Sir William Drummond's steward of Scotland attended Mountain Man Rendezvous and brought

00:40:48.740 --> 00:40:53.310
along the painter Alfred Jacob Miller.

00:40:53.310 --> 00:41:00.000
Prince Maximilian Aved, a German ethnographer, went up to Missouri to study the Indians and

00:41:00.000 --> 00:41:07.220
hired the Swiss artist Karl Bodmer to illustrate his detailed report.

00:41:07.220 --> 00:41:12.470
But Sir St. George Gore of Ireland came merely to hunt.

00:41:12.470 --> 00:41:19.730
His extravagant expedition cost him a quarter of a million dollars.

00:41:19.730 --> 00:41:24.530
He's got 50 people with him, most of them servants and skinners.

00:41:24.530 --> 00:41:30.000
He's got six wagons and 21 carts and 112 hunting horses and 50

00:41:30.000 --> 00:41:36.170
What it's all about, of course, is allowing Gore to kill as many animals as he possibly

00:41:36.170 --> 00:41:38.050
can.

00:41:38.050 --> 00:41:46.590
During his three years traversing the West, Gore killed 1,500 elk, 2,000 deer, more than

00:41:46.590 --> 00:41:55.890
1,000 antelope, 500 bears, and 4,000 bison, leaving their carcasses on the prairie, unless

00:41:55.890 --> 00:42:00.000
he considered part of the dead animal worthy of being shipped back.

00:42:00.000 --> 00:42:06.100
home as a trophy. His destruction of wildlife was so wanton, many of the

00:42:06.100 --> 00:42:12.330
frontiersmen he had hired were offended by it, and Indian tribes complained to

00:42:12.330 --> 00:42:18.920
the United States government. At the end of his three-year journey, he and his men

00:42:18.920 --> 00:42:23.540
decided they would head down to the Black Hills, which hadn't been explored by

00:42:23.540 --> 00:42:29.060
white people, sacred ground for the Lakotas. So they showed up there and they

00:42:29.060 --> 00:42:30.000
were met by a couple hundred

00:42:30.000 --> 00:42:33.110
of Lakota warriors who said, "You've got a choice.

00:42:33.110 --> 00:42:36.810
This is our sacred place. You can't be here.

00:42:36.810 --> 00:42:40.430
You either fight us or give us your guns,

00:42:40.430 --> 00:42:44.880
give us your supplies, and head the hell out of here."

00:42:44.880 --> 00:42:52.310
The West is where the American identity is.

00:42:52.310 --> 00:42:55.970
What are the things that stand distinctively for that West

00:42:55.970 --> 00:43:00.000
and therefore stand distinctively for the American people, who we are?

00:43:00.000 --> 00:43:04.780
what sets us apart from the old world and they settled more than anything else

00:43:04.780 --> 00:43:10.810
upon these two images and these two characters of the far west the American

00:43:10.810 --> 00:43:16.700
Indian and American bison the American Buffalo they became sort of the symbols

00:43:16.700 --> 00:43:24.470
of who the emerging Americans were it is a melancholy contemplation for one who

00:43:24.470 --> 00:43:29.150
has traveled as I have through these realms and have seen this noble animal

00:43:29.150 --> 00:43:30.000
and all its pride

00:43:30.000 --> 00:43:36.360
and glory to contemplate it so rapidly wasting from the world drawing the

00:43:36.360 --> 00:43:43.120
irresistible conclusion that its species is soon to be extinguished and with it

00:43:43.120 --> 00:43:47.120
the peace and happiness if not the actual existence of the tribes of Indians

00:43:47.120 --> 00:43:52.990
who are joint tenants with them in the occupancy of these vast plains George

00:43:52.990 --> 00:44:00.000
Catlin the artist George Catlin spent six years criss-crossing the way

00:44:00.000 --> 00:44:04.110
painting portraits of native people and their environment.

00:44:04.110 --> 00:44:08.080
He thrilled at joining the Lakotas in a bison hunt,

00:44:08.080 --> 00:44:14.950
but Catlins still worried that both the animals and the Indians would soon be destroyed.

00:44:14.950 --> 00:44:17.510
Then, he had a vision.

00:44:17.510 --> 00:44:21.970
And what a splendid contemplation, too,

00:44:21.970 --> 00:44:26.210
when one imagines them as they might in future be seen

00:44:26.210 --> 00:44:29.440
by some great protecting policy of government.

00:44:30.000 --> 00:44:37.850
preserved in their pristine beauty and wildness in a magnificent park, a nation's park, containing

00:44:37.850 --> 00:44:46.160
man and beast in all the wild and freshness of their nature's beauty.

00:44:46.160 --> 00:44:52.150
But on the plains, the nation's relentless movement westward was beginning to hem in the

00:44:52.150 --> 00:45:00.000
bison and the native people who relied on them.

00:45:00.000 --> 00:45:03.950
the expansion, everything had to get out of the way.

00:45:03.950 --> 00:45:08.310
You've probably seen the old painting, Manifest Destiny.

00:45:08.310 --> 00:45:11.030
They show everything fleeing in front of this horde

00:45:11.030 --> 00:45:15.450
of wagon trains and people on foot and horseback.

00:45:15.450 --> 00:45:20.190
When the Europeans come in, everything that's natural

00:45:20.190 --> 00:45:22.510
has to get out of the way.

00:45:22.510 --> 00:45:26.380
It just, it's just a matter of fact.

00:45:26.380 --> 00:45:30.000
- There is a phrase that as the settlement moved west,

00:45:30.000 --> 00:45:37.040
were redeeming the land from wilderness by the hand of man. You're redeeming the

00:45:37.040 --> 00:45:43.300
wilderness by plowing it, by cutting the trees down, by killing the wild animals

00:45:43.300 --> 00:45:50.130
and replacing them with domestic cattle or hogs. That was the mindset and that is

00:45:50.130 --> 00:45:57.110
the starkest way I can try to describe how different that was from the native

00:45:57.110 --> 00:46:00.000
people's view of it. To live with the

00:46:00.000 --> 00:46:04.580
land, that they were part of it, they weren't superior to the rest of God's

00:46:04.580 --> 00:46:12.090
creation. We saw it differently and a lot of people and a lot of animals paid a

00:46:12.090 --> 00:46:17.970
price for it. More than a million cattle and sheep had accompanied the wagon

00:46:17.970 --> 00:46:23.960
trains to California, Oregon and Santa Fe, devouring the grasses along the

00:46:23.960 --> 00:46:28.640
trails and spreading diseases like anthrax to the bison.

00:46:30.000 --> 00:46:38.340
In what is now Wyoming, the overland trails cross through the hunting grounds of the Shoshone.

00:46:38.340 --> 00:46:43.850
Since the white man has made a road across our land and has killed off our gang, we are

00:46:43.850 --> 00:46:47.840
hungry, and there is nothing for us to eat.

00:46:47.840 --> 00:46:53.920
Our women and children cry for food, and we have no food to give them.

00:46:53.920 --> 00:46:55.340
Washakie.

00:46:55.340 --> 00:47:00.000
Waves of epidemics from Europe.

00:47:00.000 --> 00:47:07.290
had also devastated Plains tribes. The Pawnee lost half of their population to

00:47:07.290 --> 00:47:14.260
smallpox. The Mandan, Assiniboine and Blackfeet were hit even harder. Only one

00:47:14.260 --> 00:47:21.550
tenth of their people survived the disease. Kiowa calendars noted one year as

00:47:21.550 --> 00:47:30.000
the smallpox winter. The summer of 1849 was remembered as the cramp Sundance in which 50

00:47:30.000 --> 00:47:36.420
percent of them died from cholera while others died by suicide in pain and

00:47:36.420 --> 00:47:45.310
despair disease came in waves when one in the family got it another and another

00:47:45.310 --> 00:47:55.790
and another and it was devastating they had a continuous grave at the same time

00:47:55.790 --> 00:48:00.000
the government was forcibly removing tens of thousands of

00:48:00.000 --> 00:48:06.510
Native Americans from their homelands in the Midwest and Southeast, including the Sauk

00:48:06.510 --> 00:48:14.410
and the Fox, and the Ottawa, the Seneca and Shawnee, the Cherokee, Choctaw, Chickasaw,

00:48:14.410 --> 00:48:21.470
Creek, and Seminole, transplanting them into a newly declared Indian territory in Kansas

00:48:21.470 --> 00:48:30.000
and Oklahoma. Some of them began hunting buffalo, too. In the Southwest, New Mexican

00:48:30.000 --> 00:48:37.640
descendants of Spanish settlers, were also making annual forays onto the Great Plains

00:48:37.640 --> 00:48:40.510
to hunt buffalo.

00:48:40.510 --> 00:48:47.580
And from Canada, the Métis, descendants of Europeans and indigenous people, were expanding

00:48:47.580 --> 00:48:53.310
their buffalo hunts across the border into the Dakotas.

00:48:53.310 --> 00:48:59.160
In 1846, a decade-long drought began, withering the grasslands.

00:49:00.000 --> 00:49:09.240
Bison herds, already pressured by the buffalo robe trade, diminished even more.

00:49:09.240 --> 00:49:16.730
A Lakota calendar commemorated a special ceremony meant to bring the buffalo back.

00:49:16.730 --> 00:49:25.690
The Kiowas prepared for a great antelope drive because the supply of bison meat was insufficient.

00:49:25.690 --> 00:49:30.000
A Blackfeet band marked 1854 as the year

00:49:30.000 --> 00:49:31.600
when we ate dogs.

00:49:34.390 --> 00:49:38.030
By the end of the 1850s, the bison had been driven

00:49:38.030 --> 00:49:40.990
from all but the interior portion of the plains,

00:49:41.640 --> 00:49:44.320
where, by the mid-1860s,

00:49:44.750 --> 00:49:49.130
an estimated 12 to 15 million of them still lived.

00:49:50.260 --> 00:49:54.170
That's a lot of bison, 12 to 15 million animals.

00:49:54.630 --> 00:49:56.010
There were still a lot of bison to hunt,

00:49:56.010 --> 00:50:00.000
and there would remain to be a lot of bison.

00:50:00.030 --> 00:50:10.670
there up until into the 1870s when the real hammer fell we saw the first train

00:50:10.670 --> 00:50:17.840
of cars that any of us had seen we looked at it from a high ridge far off

00:50:17.840 --> 00:50:24.490
it was very small but it kept coming and growing larger all the time puffing out

00:50:24.490 --> 00:50:30.000
smoke and steam as it came on we said to each other

00:50:30.320 --> 00:50:32.760
that it looked like a white man's pipe

00:50:32.760 --> 00:50:35.270
when he was smoking.

00:50:35.270 --> 00:50:40.590
Porcupine.

00:50:40.590 --> 00:50:42.130
- After the Civil War,

00:50:42.130 --> 00:50:44.490
Americans set out with renewed energy

00:50:44.490 --> 00:50:46.970
to unite the East and West,

00:50:46.970 --> 00:50:50.080
building railroads to span the continent,

00:50:50.080 --> 00:50:53.340
opening up vast areas beyond the Missouri River

00:50:53.340 --> 00:50:55.360
for homesteaders,

00:50:55.360 --> 00:50:57.040
creating easier access

00:50:57.040 --> 00:51:00.000
to distant metropolitan markets for crops,

00:51:00.060 --> 00:51:04.370
and cattle, and servicing the demands of boom towns

00:51:04.370 --> 00:51:07.130
that had sprung up after gold discoveries

00:51:07.130 --> 00:51:11.420
in the mountains of Colorado and Montana.

00:51:11.420 --> 00:51:13.410
- There are lots of technologies that move

00:51:13.410 --> 00:51:15.570
into the Great Plains in the 19th century,

00:51:15.570 --> 00:51:19.810
and most of them have a negative impact on the bison.

00:51:19.810 --> 00:51:22.620
But all of this pales in comparison

00:51:22.620 --> 00:51:26.790
to sort of spasm of industrial expansion

00:51:26.790 --> 00:51:30.000
into the Great Plains after the Civil War.

00:51:30.890 --> 00:51:35.510
Native people called this newest arrival the iron horse,

00:51:35.510 --> 00:51:39.850
and the pace of change quickened as never before.

00:51:39.850 --> 00:51:43.150
As the Union Pacific pushed west across Nebraska

00:51:43.150 --> 00:51:47.570
toward California, the Kansas Pacific aimed for Denver,

00:51:47.570 --> 00:51:50.290
piercing into the heart of the Buffalo Range

00:51:50.290 --> 00:51:53.120
of the Central Plains.

00:51:53.120 --> 00:51:56.240
To feed the hungry crews laying track,

00:51:56.240 --> 00:52:00.000
the railroad company hired an ambitious and flamboyant

00:52:00.060 --> 00:52:06.440
21-year-old Union veteran, paying him $500 a month to keep them supplied with

00:52:06.440 --> 00:52:14.560
the meat from 12 buffalo a day. His name was William F. Cody. Within a few years,

00:52:14.560 --> 00:52:21.640
he would be known by a different name. During my engagement as a hunter for the

00:52:21.640 --> 00:52:30.000
Kansas Pacific, I killed 4,280 buffalo. It was not long before I acquired

00:52:30.000 --> 00:52:36.130
a considerable reputation and the very appropriate name of Buffalo Bill was

00:52:36.130 --> 00:52:42.880
conferred upon me by the railroad hands. It has stuck with me ever since and I

00:52:42.880 --> 00:52:52.510
have never been ashamed of it. To publicize its progress across the plains, the Kansas

00:52:52.510 --> 00:52:57.970
Pacific promoted excursion trips for passengers eager to have the chance to

00:52:57.970 --> 00:53:00.000
see and shoot at

00:53:00.030 --> 00:53:03.590
the buffalo they were sure to encounter.

00:53:03.590 --> 00:53:05.890
A church group from Lawrence, Kansas,

00:53:05.890 --> 00:53:07.890
organized a two-day outing

00:53:07.890 --> 00:53:10.570
to raise money for the congregation.

00:53:10.570 --> 00:53:13.400
300 people signed up.

00:53:13.400 --> 00:53:18.030
On the second day, they came upon a herd.

00:53:18.030 --> 00:53:20.190
The buffalo kept pace with the train

00:53:20.190 --> 00:53:22.420
for at least a quarter of a mile,

00:53:22.420 --> 00:53:26.380
while the boys blazed away at them without effect.

00:53:26.380 --> 00:53:29.920
Shots enough were fired to rout a regimen of men.

00:53:31.020 --> 00:53:37.000
The train stopped, and such a scrambling and screeching was never before heard on the plains.

00:53:37.120 --> 00:53:44.820
As we rushed forth to see our first game lying in his gore, I had the pleasure of first putting

00:53:44.820 --> 00:53:51.780
hands on the dark locks of the noble monster who had fallen so bravely. Then came the ladies.

00:53:52.650 --> 00:54:00.000
A ring was formed. The cornet band gathered around and played Yankee Doodle. I thought that

00:54:00.000 --> 00:54:07.540
Hail to the chief would have done more honor to the departed.

00:54:07.540 --> 00:54:10.990
When the white men wanted to build railroads,

00:54:10.990 --> 00:54:14.440
or when they wanted to farm or raise cattle,

00:54:14.440 --> 00:54:17.960
the buffalo protected the Kiowas.

00:54:17.960 --> 00:54:20.860
They tore up the railroad tracks and the gardens.

00:54:20.860 --> 00:54:24.250
They chased the cattle off the ranges.

00:54:24.250 --> 00:54:28.800
The buffalo loved their people as much as the Kiowas loved them.

00:54:30.220 --> 00:54:39.550
old lady horse. For decades, native tribes had resisted incursions onto their homelands,

00:54:39.550 --> 00:54:45.330
and the army had built forts in response. Now more forts were established, and more

00:54:45.330 --> 00:54:53.300
troops were dispatched to man them. Indian warriors attacked survey crews and road gangs,

00:54:53.300 --> 00:55:00.000
sometimes even derailed trains. The army's retaliations were ineffective, and in 1880,

00:55:00.000 --> 00:55:05.350
In 1967, Congress decided to try a different approach.

00:55:05.350 --> 00:55:11.660
Delegations were dispatched to pursue what some called the hitherto untried policy of

00:55:11.660 --> 00:55:14.980
conquering with kindness.

00:55:14.980 --> 00:55:22.750
That October, more than 5,000 Kiowas, Comanches, Arapahoes and Southern Cheyennes gathered at

00:55:22.750 --> 00:55:30.000
Medicine Lodge Creek in Kansas to hear a proposal from U.S. officials intended to end the violence

00:55:30.000 --> 00:55:32.780
on the Southern Plains.

00:55:32.780 --> 00:55:34.420
Under the government's plan,

00:55:34.420 --> 00:55:37.560
the United States would encourage white settlement

00:55:37.560 --> 00:55:41.040
north of the Arkansas River.

00:55:41.040 --> 00:55:43.560
The Indians would move onto reservations

00:55:43.560 --> 00:55:45.940
in what is now Oklahoma,

00:55:45.940 --> 00:55:49.970
where they could receive food and supplies for 30 years,

00:55:49.970 --> 00:55:52.700
be provided schools for their children,

00:55:52.700 --> 00:55:56.020
and taught how to farm.

00:55:56.020 --> 00:55:59.350
The Kiowa chief, Satanta, objected.

00:56:00.890 --> 00:56:09.220
I want you to understand what I say. Write it on paper. I don't want to settle. I love

00:56:09.220 --> 00:56:16.320
to roam over the prairies. There I feel free and happy. But when we settle down, we grow

00:56:16.320 --> 00:56:22.600
pale and die." "Do not ask us to give up the buffalo

00:56:22.600 --> 00:56:30.000
for the sheep," Ten Bears of the Comanche added. "Do not speak of it more. The peace

00:56:30.000 --> 00:56:36.740
Commissioners promised that south of the Arkansas, non-Indians would be prohibited from settlement

00:56:36.740 --> 00:56:43.680
and the tribes could continue hunting there so long, the treaty said, as the buffalo may range

00:56:43.680 --> 00:56:51.460
there in such numbers as justify the chase. Though not every band of each tribe was represented,

00:56:52.150 --> 00:57:00.000
the treaty was signed and sent to Congress. The Kiowa calendar for that year showed an Indian and a white

00:57:00.000 --> 00:57:03.590
man shaking hands near a grove of trees.

00:57:03.590 --> 00:57:09.910
The government comes away from that treaty thinking that it has set in motion this transformation

00:57:09.910 --> 00:57:17.410
of Indian peoples from hunting, gathering semi-pneumatic people to farmers.

00:57:17.410 --> 00:57:21.710
The Comanches and Kiowas come away from that treaty thinking that they have now permission

00:57:21.710 --> 00:57:28.090
to continue doing what they have always done and therefore achieving absolutely nothing.

00:57:28.090 --> 00:57:30.000
A year later.

00:57:30.350 --> 00:57:33.900
Farther north, at Fort Laramie on the Platte,

00:57:33.900 --> 00:57:38.170
a similar treaty was signed by some of the Lakota Sioux.

00:57:38.170 --> 00:57:41.960
In exchange for the government abandoning its army forts

00:57:41.960 --> 00:57:44.380
in Wyoming's Powder River country,

00:57:44.380 --> 00:57:47.360
a vast Sioux reservation was created,

00:57:47.360 --> 00:57:50.790
encompassing half of present-day South Dakota,

00:57:50.790 --> 00:57:54.150
including the sacred Black Hills.

00:57:54.150 --> 00:57:57.830
The treaty also contained a clause stating

00:57:57.830 --> 00:58:00.000
the Lakotas were free to hunt out

00:58:00.030 --> 00:58:07.870
the reservation so long as there were buffalo. General William Tecumseh Sherman, now in command

00:58:07.870 --> 00:58:14.510
of the army in the West, reluctantly agreed to the hunting concession. This may lead to collisions,

00:58:14.750 --> 00:58:20.130
Sherman wrote his brother, but it will not be long before all the buffaloes are extinct,

00:58:20.130 --> 00:58:23.430
near and between the railroads.

00:58:31.150 --> 00:58:37.120
"We want to go on the buffalo hunt, so long as there are any buffaloes.

00:58:37.120 --> 00:58:42.450
We are afraid, when we have no meat to offer the Great Spirit,

00:58:42.450 --> 00:58:46.490
he will be angry and punish us.

00:58:46.490 --> 00:58:49.580
Those buffalo are mine."

00:58:49.580 --> 00:58:53.080
Beta Nasharo.

00:58:53.080 --> 00:58:58.490
In 1872, a hunt took place in southwestern Nebraska.

00:58:58.490 --> 00:59:00.000
Under the government's peace policy,

00:59:00.000 --> 00:59:05.210
policy, the Pawnees had also been placed on a reservation, but were given permission to

00:59:05.210 --> 00:59:11.060
leave it in their annual search for bison herds, provided they were chaperoned by white

00:59:11.060 --> 00:59:16.640
men whose job was to make sure there were no troubles with settlers now living on the

00:59:16.640 --> 00:59:20.890
Pawnee's old homelands.

00:59:20.890 --> 00:59:28.200
Joining the hunt was a 22-year-old son of a prominent Wall Street banker, George "Bird"

00:59:30.440 --> 00:59:34.820
As a student at Yale, he had ventured west for the first time

00:59:34.820 --> 00:59:37.720
as part of a paleontology expedition

00:59:37.720 --> 00:59:40.860
that unearthed the bones of extinct animals,

00:59:40.860 --> 00:59:45.230
including a pterodactyl and a tiny Eohippus,

00:59:45.230 --> 00:59:49.330
the world's first known horse.

00:59:49.330 --> 00:59:53.070
He has this incredibly hands-on, tangible experience

00:59:53.070 --> 00:59:57.830
where they discover 100 extinct species.

00:59:57.830 --> 01:00:00.000
So for somebody of that era, he

01:00:00.000 --> 01:00:05.660
understands in a very unique way that extinction is something that's possible.

01:00:05.660 --> 01:00:12.010
Now the Pawnees introduced Grinnell to some of their sacred rituals before going after

01:00:12.010 --> 01:00:13.810
the bison.

01:00:13.810 --> 01:00:19.480
The success of the hunt, he wrote, was supposed to depend largely upon the respect shown to

01:00:19.480 --> 01:00:21.970
the buffalo.

01:00:21.970 --> 01:00:30.000
He marveled at how disciplined the Pawnee hunters were, how skillfully they handled their horses,

01:00:30.000 --> 01:00:35.800
the tribe celebrated after the successful hunt.

01:00:35.840 --> 01:00:40.440
That night, when he's sitting around the campfire with a Pawnee,

01:00:40.480 --> 01:00:46.360
he has this epiphany, and Grinnell was somebody who throughout his life

01:00:46.380 --> 01:00:50.530
could see what was coming before most other people.

01:00:50.550 --> 01:00:55.190
Their days are numbered, and unless some action on this subject

01:00:55.230 --> 01:01:00.000
is speedily taken, not only by the states and territories,

01:01:00.000 --> 01:01:07.480
government, these shaggy brown beasts, these cattle upon a thousand hills, will

01:01:07.480 --> 01:01:25.100
ere long be among the things of the past. In the fall of 1872, tracks for a new

01:01:25.100 --> 01:01:30.000
railroad, the Atchison, Topeka, and Santa Fe reached a small

01:01:30.120 --> 01:01:37.660
settlement that had grown up around Fort Dodge on the north shore of the Arkansas River.

01:01:37.660 --> 01:01:42.850
Town builders had originally hoped to name it Buffalo City, but the Postal Service turned

01:01:42.850 --> 01:01:48.220
them down since Kansas already had a town by that name.

01:01:48.220 --> 01:01:53.950
So they christened it Dodge City in honor of the nearby fort.

01:01:53.950 --> 01:02:00.000
The first construction train to arrive was delayed two hours, waiting for a bison herd

01:02:00.160 --> 01:02:07.420
three miles long to pass in front of it. Buffalo often grazed so close to Dodge City,

01:02:07.990 --> 01:02:15.360
one merchant shot them from the fence of his corral for his hogs to feed on. But news from

01:02:15.360 --> 01:02:23.790
the east was about to transform life on the southern plains yet again. Commercial tanners

01:02:23.790 --> 01:02:30.000
in Europe, England, and Philadelphia had developed a way to efficiently process stiff

01:02:30.000 --> 01:02:37.790
buffalo hides into a supple but durable leather, as good as a cow's hide, and especially suitable

01:02:37.790 --> 01:02:42.320
for the belts used to drive industrial machines.

01:02:42.320 --> 01:02:44.460
There's a shortage of leather.

01:02:44.460 --> 01:02:47.720
Leather is the fifth largest industry in the United States.

01:02:47.720 --> 01:02:51.840
And so one of the reasons why this industrial society reaches out into the Great Plains

01:02:51.840 --> 01:03:00.000
to consume bison hides is just to feed this appetite for leather.

01:03:00.000 --> 01:03:05.480
for as many hides as they could get, and offered more than $3 for each one.

01:03:05.480 --> 01:03:13.940
A young Vermonter named J. Wright Moore brought in 305 hides and made more than $1,000 in

01:03:13.940 --> 01:03:21.690
a month's time, nearly twice what an average day worker back east made in a year.

01:03:21.690 --> 01:03:27.810
Word that there was money to be made in hides spread quickly, and soon more men flocked

01:03:27.810 --> 01:03:30.000
to Dodge City.

01:03:30.000 --> 01:03:38.740
of them according to one newspaper each dreaming of striking it rich the whole

01:03:38.740 --> 01:03:46.860
Western country went buffalo wild it was like a gold rush men left jobs

01:03:46.860 --> 01:03:54.480
businesses wives and children there were uncounted millions of the beasts they

01:03:54.480 --> 01:03:59.280
didn't belong to anybody if you could kill them what they brought was yours

01:04:00.000 --> 01:04:03.780
They were like walking gold pieces.

01:04:03.780 --> 01:04:05.970
Frank Mayer.

01:04:05.970 --> 01:04:09.580
Frank Mayer from Pennsylvania sank everything

01:04:09.580 --> 01:04:11.890
he owned into a hunting outfit--

01:04:11.890 --> 01:04:17.260
wagons, mules, camp equipment, and firearms.

01:04:17.260 --> 01:04:19.320
They called themselves Buffalo Runners

01:04:19.320 --> 01:04:23.310
because Buffalo Runner was kind of a romantic term

01:04:23.310 --> 01:04:28.060
that kind of suggested that there was some fair chase,

01:04:28.060 --> 01:04:30.000
sort of fair fight going.

01:04:30.000 --> 01:04:36.420
on. The notion of galloping up on a trusty steed beside a charging buffalo and

01:04:36.420 --> 01:04:41.950
killing it was the romantic notion of how buffalo were killed. That's very

01:04:41.950 --> 01:04:47.830
different, of course, from how commercial hunting actually worked. For newcomers,

01:04:47.830 --> 01:04:52.750
Frank Mayer said, shooting from the back of a running horse was always uncertain.

01:04:52.750 --> 01:05:00.000
It meant too many wasted shots, too many wounded buffalo, too many carcasses of whatever

01:05:00.000 --> 01:05:05.990
bison he managed to kill scattered across greater distances. And the rifles

01:05:05.990 --> 01:05:11.430
they used often required several shots to bring a buffalo down. "I was a

01:05:11.430 --> 01:05:19.470
businessman," Mayer said. "I wanted efficiency." One hunter wrote a letter to

01:05:19.470 --> 01:05:24.010
the Sharps Rifle Manufacturing Company in Connecticut, asking for a

01:05:24.010 --> 01:05:30.000
better gun. Sharps responded with a series of new models, as did other

01:05:30.000 --> 01:05:32.990
manufacturers.

01:05:32.990 --> 01:05:39.650
The rifles weighed 12 to 16 pounds, had longer and wider barrels that could handle larger

01:05:39.650 --> 01:05:46.270
amounts of gunpowder to fire heavier slugs of lead with great accuracy over a distance

01:05:46.270 --> 01:05:55.550
of 400 yards, even reached targets more than 1,000 yards away.

01:05:55.550 --> 01:05:59.350
The most effective killing technique was called a stand.

01:06:00.350 --> 01:06:06.870
A hunter carefully positioned himself about 200 to 300 yards downwind from the herd.

01:06:07.920 --> 01:06:15.050
Then he picked out a lead buffalo, took careful aim, and fired, usually shooting for the lungs.

01:06:16.990 --> 01:06:19.730
They don't shoot for the shoulder, they shoot it through the lungs.

01:06:20.360 --> 01:06:23.140
When you shoot it through the lungs, it would tend to not move far.

01:06:23.140 --> 01:06:27.530
It would stand there, get woozy, fall over dead.

01:06:28.260 --> 01:06:30.000
The other animals see the lead animals.

01:06:30.000 --> 01:06:34.210
laying there and they don't want to move now if they started to drift you'd shoot

01:06:34.210 --> 01:06:39.770
whoever is out in the lead of that drift anything you can do to not induce panic

01:06:39.770 --> 01:06:46.130
and they would just whittle away at these things another one goes down they

01:06:46.130 --> 01:06:53.020
mill around and they're still not spooked this was short-circuiting ten thousand

01:06:53.020 --> 01:07:00.000
years of defense mechanism that had evolved over time native people who saw the new

01:07:00.000 --> 01:07:07.450
Buffalo guns in action said, "It shoots today and kills tomorrow."

01:07:07.450 --> 01:07:12.480
Kills tomorrow? That's exactly what was happening.

01:07:12.480 --> 01:07:17.810
It was killing tomorrow for the bison and for the people who relied on it.

01:07:17.810 --> 01:07:24.870
The white men hired hunters to do nothing but kill the buffalo.

01:07:24.870 --> 01:07:29.800
Up and down the plains, these men ranged.

01:07:30.000 --> 01:07:34.740
shooting, sometimes as many as a hundred a day.

01:07:34.740 --> 01:07:39.060
Behind them came the skinners with their wagons.

01:07:39.060 --> 01:07:44.150
They piled hides into the wagons until they were full

01:07:44.150 --> 01:07:49.550
and then took their loads to the railroad stations.

01:07:49.550 --> 01:07:51.610
- It was a harvest.

01:07:51.610 --> 01:07:54.280
We were the harvesters.

01:07:54.280 --> 01:07:57.150
We never killed all the buff we could,

01:07:57.150 --> 01:07:59.960
but only as many as our skinners could handle.

01:08:01.850 --> 01:08:08.380
The Skinners went to work, stripping the hide off the carcasses from the neck down.

01:08:08.380 --> 01:08:12.400
Some outfits also took some of the meat for sale.

01:08:12.400 --> 01:08:19.850
Most just removed the tongues, worth 25 cents each, and left everything else, six to eight

01:08:19.850 --> 01:08:30.000
hundred pounds of meat, along with the hooves and the head and the horns, to rot.

01:08:30.000 --> 01:08:37.600
of buffalo the year before, there were now myriads of carcasses. The air was foul with

01:08:37.600 --> 01:08:43.610
a sickening stench, and the vast plain, which only a short 12 months before teemed with

01:08:43.610 --> 01:08:54.500
animal life, was a dead, solitary, putrid desert. Colonel Richard Irving Dodge.

01:08:54.500 --> 01:08:59.760
This is the Industrial Revolution arriving on the magnificent Great Plains.

01:09:00.320 --> 01:09:05.550
they were turning it into a factory floor.

01:09:05.550 --> 01:09:08.250
You know, instead of assembling something, though,

01:09:08.250 --> 01:09:10.890
they were disassembling something.

01:09:10.890 --> 01:09:12.970
They were disassembling an animal

01:09:12.970 --> 01:09:16.980
and just taking a certain part of it and leaving the rest.

01:09:16.980 --> 01:09:19.760
And then the conveyor belt was the railroads

01:09:19.760 --> 01:09:21.990
that would take the disassembled part

01:09:21.990 --> 01:09:26.110
back to run a machine on the East Coast.

01:09:26.110 --> 01:09:27.330
It was a factory.

01:09:27.330 --> 01:09:30.000
And the buffalo hunters, whatever we

01:09:30.000 --> 01:09:36.730
might want to think about them, they were in essence, you know, they were factory workers.

01:09:36.730 --> 01:09:47.040
It had this metronomic industrial beat to it, relentless, relentless, relentless.

01:09:47.040 --> 01:09:52.300
More than a million hides made their way east from the southern plains before the end of

01:09:52.300 --> 01:09:54.970
1873.

01:09:54.970 --> 01:09:59.670
Even that number did not account for the actual damage to the bison.

01:10:00.350 --> 01:10:02.690
If they shot the buffalo more than once,

01:10:02.690 --> 01:10:04.250
that could destroy the hide.

01:10:04.250 --> 01:10:06.330
If the skinners were not good at their work,

01:10:06.330 --> 01:10:08.390
the skinners could destroy the hide.

01:10:08.390 --> 01:10:11.130
If the hides were staked out and there was a rainstorm,

01:10:11.130 --> 01:10:12.470
the hides could rot.

01:10:12.470 --> 01:10:15.570
Insects came along and chewed on the hides.

01:10:15.570 --> 01:10:19.970
So by one estimate, you had to kill about four buffalo

01:10:19.970 --> 01:10:22.920
to get one hide to market.

01:10:22.920 --> 01:10:24.800
So not only was the carcass wasted,

01:10:24.800 --> 01:10:28.770
but even the hides were wasted in this industry.

01:10:30.440 --> 01:10:35.060
Uncounted numbers of wounded buffalo wandered off and died.

01:10:35.060 --> 01:10:37.870
So, did motherless calves.

01:10:37.870 --> 01:10:41.860
It was a business proposition for them.

01:10:41.860 --> 01:10:44.430
A hide's a hide.

01:10:44.430 --> 01:10:49.280
If you shoot the mother of a calf, what the hell.

01:10:49.280 --> 01:10:55.450
But, those calves, I would venture, every one of them died.

01:10:55.450 --> 01:10:59.160
They don't survive if they don't have their mother.

01:11:00.280 --> 01:11:10.070
it was ugly, ugly business. With 5,000 rifles a day leveled at him, it wasn't long till there

01:11:10.070 --> 01:11:20.140
was very little of him or her left to shoot. Within a year or a year and a half after I got into the

01:11:20.140 --> 01:11:30.000
business, we hit what I now know is called diminishing returns. We called it a scarcity of buffalo. And my dreams of

01:11:30.000 --> 01:11:39.910
of fortune. They grew dimmer and dimmer as the months went by. Frank Mayer.

01:11:39.910 --> 01:11:47.500
The hide yards at Dodge City now stood empty, but south of the Arkansas River, in the area

01:11:47.500 --> 01:11:54.280
reserved by the Medicine Lodge Treaty, solely for native hunting, massive herds of buffalo

01:11:54.280 --> 01:11:59.960
still roamed. The Vermonter Jay Wright Moore crossed over to investigate.

01:12:01.020 --> 01:12:04.850
For five days, he said, we rode through and camped

01:12:04.850 --> 01:12:09.680
in a mobile sea of living buffalo.

01:12:09.680 --> 01:12:13.810
Back at Fort Dodge, he and other hunters asked the commander

01:12:13.810 --> 01:12:16.730
what the army would do if they trespassed

01:12:16.730 --> 01:12:19.130
onto the treaty lands.

01:12:19.130 --> 01:12:22.590
If I were a buffalo hunter, the officer replied,

01:12:22.590 --> 01:12:27.090
I would hunt buffalo where the buffalo are.

01:12:27.090 --> 01:12:30.000
- The military in the West certainly had a motive

01:12:30.380 --> 01:12:35.520
to do what they could to eliminate as many bison as they could.

01:12:35.520 --> 01:12:41.000
Because they understood the obvious that the bison were a key to the native economy.

01:12:41.000 --> 01:12:44.760
If you cut the leg cement of that economy, then you're not going to have much resistance

01:12:44.760 --> 01:12:46.100
from native people.

01:12:46.100 --> 01:12:49.240
The Army was a facilitator in the destruction of the bison.

01:12:49.240 --> 01:12:54.190
They didn't do it themselves, but they certainly helped it and supported it.

01:12:54.190 --> 01:13:00.000
I think it was a deliberate policy of the U.S. government for the bison to be destroyed.

01:13:00.000 --> 01:13:07.060
not something that they wrote down and propagated through legislation, but I think through all

01:13:07.060 --> 01:13:13.870
sorts of informal practices and lots of winking and nudging, the destruction of the buffalo

01:13:13.870 --> 01:13:17.680
was something that was very much encouraged.

01:13:17.680 --> 01:13:22.920
The Arkansas was called the deadline, south of which no hunter should go, but as buffalo

01:13:22.920 --> 01:13:27.280
grew fewer in number, we gazed longingly across the sandy wastes that marked the course of

01:13:27.280 --> 01:13:29.070
that river.

01:13:29.070 --> 01:13:30.000
The oftener we looked.

01:13:30.320 --> 01:13:33.210
the more eager we became to tempt fate,

01:13:33.210 --> 01:13:36.600
even the sky looked more inviting in that direction.

01:13:36.600 --> 01:13:44.560
So, we crossed over Billy Dixon.

01:13:44.560 --> 01:13:51.250
- There are a couple of Army officers

01:13:51.250 --> 01:13:53.730
who write to people in the East,

01:13:53.730 --> 01:13:56.800
and they say, "Look, we've got to put a stop to this.

01:13:56.800 --> 01:14:00.000
"You're causing a lot of misery," which may just

01:14:00.000 --> 01:14:05.010
create more violence than solving the problem of violence

01:14:05.010 --> 01:14:06.710
in the Great Plains.

01:14:06.710 --> 01:14:11.710
In early 1874, Congressman Greenbury Lafayette Fort

01:14:11.710 --> 01:14:16.130
of Illinois proposed legislation making it unlawful

01:14:16.130 --> 01:14:20.580
for any person who is not an Indian to kill, wound,

01:14:20.580 --> 01:14:25.670
or in any way destroy any female buffalo of any age

01:14:25.670 --> 01:14:27.980
found at large within the boundaries

01:14:27.980 --> 01:14:30.000
of the territories of the United States.

01:14:31.980 --> 01:14:37.180
Reformers like Representative Fort had been galvanized by reports of the slaughter

01:14:37.180 --> 01:14:43.790
underway on the Southern Plains. The American Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to Animals

01:14:43.790 --> 01:14:50.200
was also campaigning for something to be done. But the Secretary of the Interior,

01:14:50.670 --> 01:14:54.830
Columbus Delano, had already made his position clear.

01:14:55.500 --> 01:15:00.000
I would not seriously regret the total disappearance of the buffalo from the

01:15:00.000 --> 01:15:05.380
Western prairies in its effect on the Indians, regarding it rather as a means

01:15:05.380 --> 01:15:09.800
of hastening their sense of dependence upon the products of the soil and their

01:15:09.800 --> 01:15:16.220
own labors. When there was a desire to connect the East Coast and the West Coast,

01:15:16.220 --> 01:15:21.760
there were two great impediments. One was bison, the other was indigenous people,

01:15:21.760 --> 01:15:27.770
and they thought they could solve the second by eliminating the first. It was

01:15:27.770 --> 01:15:28.740
kind of a twofer.

01:15:30.120 --> 01:15:37.530
Arguing against the Bill's passage, Congressman James Garfield of Ohio said, "It may be possible

01:15:37.530 --> 01:15:43.030
that in our mercy to the buffalo, we may be cruel to the Indian."

01:15:43.030 --> 01:15:47.540
Eliminating the herds, he added, would be the best thing which could happen for the

01:15:47.540 --> 01:15:51.390
betterment of our Indian question.

01:15:51.390 --> 01:15:54.510
To Congressman Fort, that argument was absurd.

01:15:54.510 --> 01:16:00.000
"I am not in favor," he said, "of civilizing the Indian by starting

01:16:00.000 --> 01:16:07.320
him to death. In the end, the House passed the Buffalo Protection Bill and sent it to

01:16:07.320 --> 01:16:11.940
the Senate, which also voted in favor of it.

01:16:11.940 --> 01:16:18.220
I'm actually surprised the bill passed, given the times, but it did. And it went to Grant's

01:16:18.220 --> 01:16:24.490
desk, and of course Grant would be listening to his Secretary of the Interior, and so he

01:16:24.490 --> 01:16:29.890
didn't actually veto it, but Congress recessed, and so with not signing it, he, in effect,

01:16:30.000 --> 01:16:32.760
killed the bill.

01:16:32.760 --> 01:16:42.040
It was clear the American government would not defend the American Buffalo.

01:16:42.040 --> 01:16:48.420
It doesn't really matter whether it was an official policy or a secret policy or no policy

01:16:48.420 --> 01:16:50.110
at all.

01:16:50.110 --> 01:16:57.330
It had the same effect for the bison who were eliminated and for the people who for thousands

01:16:57.330 --> 01:17:00.000
of years had depended on those animals.

01:17:01.150 --> 01:17:06.400
The US government made treaties with the Indians

01:17:06.400 --> 01:17:10.930
when they wanted something and it was convenient.

01:17:10.930 --> 01:17:15.190
And the second that the treaty was inconvenient

01:17:15.190 --> 01:17:18.480
and they wanted something else, they broke the treaty.

01:17:18.480 --> 01:17:22.660
And that pattern permeates the history

01:17:22.660 --> 01:17:28.040
of the United States government with indigenous peoples.

01:17:28.040 --> 01:17:29.860
- In 1874,

01:17:30.000 --> 01:17:31.000
Things got worse.

01:17:31.000 --> 01:17:37.670
Lieutenant Colonel George Armstrong Custer led an expedition into the Black Hills, an

01:17:37.670 --> 01:17:44.470
area considered sacred by the Lakota and reserved exclusively for them by treaty.

01:17:44.470 --> 01:17:50.260
A prospector Custer brought along started searching for gold there.

01:17:50.260 --> 01:17:56.610
Meanwhile, farther south, hide hunters continued to cross the Arkansas River into the Buffalo

01:17:56.610 --> 01:17:59.700
Range, supposedly off-limits to whites.

01:18:00.440 --> 01:18:06.160
brazenly established outposts to keep themselves supplied with ammunition and

01:18:06.160 --> 01:18:12.930
whatever else they needed to continue their deadly business your people make

01:18:12.930 --> 01:18:18.730
big talk and sometimes make war if an Indian kills a white man's ox to keep

01:18:18.730 --> 01:18:23.950
his wife and children from starving what do you think my people ought to do

01:18:23.950 --> 01:18:30.000
when they see their cattle the buffalo killed by your race when they have

01:18:30.000 --> 01:18:38.130
are not hungry, Little Robe. The Indians sensed that we were taking away their

01:18:38.130 --> 01:18:43.180
birthright and that with every boom of a buffalo rifle their tenure on their

01:18:43.180 --> 01:18:47.840
homeland became weakened and that eventually they would have no homeland

01:18:47.840 --> 01:18:54.490
and no buffalo. So they did what you and I would do if our existence were

01:18:54.490 --> 01:18:59.670
jeopardized. They fought Frank Mayer.

01:19:01.440 --> 01:19:08.180
Incensed by the treaty violations in the southern and northern plains, warriors from the Lakota,

01:19:08.180 --> 01:19:16.740
Cheyenne, Arapaho, Kiowa, and Comanche struck back, raiding stagecoaches, wagon trains,

01:19:16.740 --> 01:19:22.340
and homesteads. Among the Quahada band of Comanches was a

01:19:22.340 --> 01:19:30.000
tall 26-year-old who was already rising in leadership, named Quanah. He had been born

01:19:30.000 --> 01:19:36.820
the sacred Wichita Mountains, the oldest son of a prominent chief and a white woman, Cynthia

01:19:36.820 --> 01:19:45.270
Ann Parker, who had been taken captive as a child and adopted into the Comanche tribe.

01:19:45.270 --> 01:19:52.260
In 1860, while Quanah and his father and most of the other warriors were gone, Texas Rangers

01:19:52.260 --> 01:19:58.370
overran their village, killed a number of people, and took his mother and baby sister

01:19:58.370 --> 01:19:59.600
into custody.

01:20:00.730 --> 01:20:06.410
It was her massacre, but it wasn't a famous thing you read about in Texas history.

01:20:06.410 --> 01:20:12.330
They eventually took her back to her people, but she didn't want to go.

01:20:12.330 --> 01:20:16.710
She never wanted to go back because she was Comanche.

01:20:16.710 --> 01:20:22.620
Cynthia tried several times to rejoin the Comanches without success.

01:20:22.620 --> 01:20:25.570
She lost her young daughter to pneumonia.

01:20:25.570 --> 01:20:30.000
Unable to live among her people, Cynthia died in despair.

01:20:32.430 --> 01:20:38.160
Her son Kwana had already distinguished himself with his fearless courage, leading attacks

01:20:38.160 --> 01:20:46.690
on Texans, against whom he harbored an implacable hatred for kidnapping his mother and sister.

01:20:46.690 --> 01:20:52.340
He had attended the Medicine Lodge treaty negotiations, which the Quahadas had adamantly

01:20:52.340 --> 01:20:55.430
refused to sign.

01:20:55.430 --> 01:21:00.000
For seven years they had stayed away from the reservation, and Kwana took part in

01:21:00.000 --> 01:21:04.330
in skirmishes with the soldiers sent to force them in.

01:21:04.330 --> 01:21:06.880
Now, at the yearly sun dance,

01:21:06.880 --> 01:21:11.830
a war against the hide hunters was being planned.

01:21:11.830 --> 01:21:16.910
- Kwana knew that they had to destroy the buffalo hunters.

01:21:18.410 --> 01:21:20.720
It becomes a matter of defense,

01:21:20.720 --> 01:21:24.880
of defending your people, of defending your family,

01:21:24.880 --> 01:21:26.960
of defending the buffalo.

01:21:26.960 --> 01:21:29.700
- A Comanche medicine man named Ishatai

01:21:30.000 --> 01:21:38.220
Announced that in a vision he had been given special powers to help the tribes retake their homelands and restore the old ways

01:21:42.760 --> 01:21:45.580
Eshittite was making big talk at that time

01:21:45.860 --> 01:21:55.470
He says God told me we are going to kill lots of white men. I will stop the bullets in their guns

01:21:56.820 --> 01:21:59.730
bullets will not pierce our shirts and

01:22:00.890 --> 01:22:02.330
We will kill them all.

01:22:03.700 --> 01:22:15.990
With Kwanna and Ishti leading, more than 300 Comanche, Kiowa, and Cheyenne set off for adobe walls.

01:22:15.990 --> 01:22:22.350
A trading post in the Texas panhandle servicing the buffalo hunters who were trespassing.

01:22:24.400 --> 01:22:29.860
29 people were there when the Indians attacked at dawn on June 27th.

01:22:30.000 --> 01:22:36.990
Two white men were killed in the early moments as hide hunters who had been sleeping under

01:22:36.990 --> 01:22:43.460
their wagons scrambled to defend themselves before taking shelter in the buildings.

01:22:43.460 --> 01:22:46.060
Billy Dixon helped drive off the attack.

01:22:46.060 --> 01:22:51.640
For the first half hour, the Indians were reckless and daring enough to ride up and

01:22:51.640 --> 01:22:53.910
strike the doors with the butts of their guns.

01:22:53.910 --> 01:23:00.000
Finally, the buffalo hunters all got straightened out and were firing with deadly effect.

01:23:00.990 --> 01:23:05.600
The Indians stood up against us for a while, but gradually began falling back

01:23:05.600 --> 01:23:11.160
as we were emptying Rawhi saddles entirely too fast for Indian safety.

01:23:11.160 --> 01:23:17.640
Seeing a group of Indians on a bluff more than three-quarters of a mile away,

01:23:17.640 --> 01:23:23.150
the hunters urged Dixon to take a shot with his Big Sharps buffalo rifle.

01:23:23.150 --> 01:23:26.780
"I took careful aim and pulled the trigger," he said.

01:23:26.780 --> 01:23:29.600
"We saw an Indian fall from his horse."

01:23:30.440 --> 01:23:35.620
The bullet had struck before the rider heard the sound of Dickson's rifle.

01:23:35.620 --> 01:23:41.100
Fifteen warriors had died in the initial attack.

01:23:41.100 --> 01:23:44.560
Kwana was wounded, but kept fighting.

01:23:44.560 --> 01:23:49.040
All the Cheyennes were very mad at Ishitai, Kwana remembered.

01:23:49.040 --> 01:23:52.780
They shouted, "What's the matter with your medicine?"

01:23:52.780 --> 01:23:57.790
One Cheyenne beat him with a riding whip.

01:23:57.790 --> 01:24:00.000
After the Battle of Adobe Walls,

01:24:00.220 --> 01:24:08.490
Comanche, Kiowa, Cheyenne, and Arapaho warriors regrouped and embarked on new raids across Texas,

01:24:08.940 --> 01:24:15.090
Colorado, and parts of New Mexico and Kansas that left 190 white people dead.

01:24:17.000 --> 01:24:20.580
President Grant put the reservations under military control.

01:24:21.540 --> 01:24:30.000
Any Indians who did not return were to be considered hostile and hunted down. On the morning of

01:24:30.000 --> 01:24:39.540
September 28, 1874, Colonel Ronald S. McKenzie and 13 companies of cavalry and infantry reached

01:24:39.540 --> 01:24:47.280
the rim of Palo Duro Canyon in the Texas Panhandle. Peering down, he saw an array of encampments

01:24:47.280 --> 01:24:54.290
spread along the canyon floor. He ordered his men down a narrow trail, and they began

01:24:54.290 --> 01:25:00.000
their charge. The villagers fled up the canyon walls, while warriors,

01:25:00.060 --> 01:25:02.470
covered their retreat.

01:25:02.470 --> 01:25:06.290
- Not many people died in the Battle of Palo Duro Canyon,

01:25:06.290 --> 01:25:10.480
but what McKenzie was able to do was they had left

01:25:10.480 --> 01:25:15.170
their teepees, their winter food supplies,

01:25:15.170 --> 01:25:17.040
and the horse herd.

01:25:17.040 --> 01:25:21.060
And he gathered up the food supplies and the teepees,

01:25:21.060 --> 01:25:24.220
set them on fire.

01:25:24.220 --> 01:25:29.120
- Then he takes this pony herd of 1,450 horses.

01:25:30.000 --> 01:25:36.530
He lets his Indian auxiliaries have the pick of about a hundred and fifty of those horses.

01:25:36.530 --> 01:25:42.830
And then he has his forces shoot down all the remaining animals.

01:25:42.830 --> 01:25:47.130
It was kind of a scorched earth strategy, "I'm not going to keep these horses, we're

01:25:47.130 --> 01:25:48.460
just going to kill them."

01:25:48.460 --> 01:25:56.400
We have elders today who say that if you go to that site, that you can still hear.

01:25:56.400 --> 01:26:00.000
You can still hear those horses and the destruction.

01:26:00.000 --> 01:26:07.230
destruction and the crime that went forth so long ago.

01:26:07.230 --> 01:26:12.950
For the rest of the fall and into the winter, the Army's columns patrolled the panhandle,

01:26:12.950 --> 01:26:19.280
ceaselessly pursuing any straggling bands who didn't return to the reservation. Many

01:26:19.280 --> 01:26:30.000
of them, reduced to eating roots and rodents to survive, began to starve. In February of 1875, the last of the

01:26:30.000 --> 01:26:37.420
was, came into the reservation at Fort Sill, Oklahoma, then the Cheyenne in March, followed

01:26:37.420 --> 01:26:46.700
by some Comanches. By May, only Quanah and his 400 Quahadas, who still had some horses,

01:26:46.700 --> 01:26:47.670
remained free.

01:26:47.670 --> 01:26:58.650
It's said that Quanah went up on a hill and drew a buffalo robe over his head and was

01:26:58.650 --> 01:27:00.000
waiting for signs.

01:27:00.000 --> 01:27:07.040
for direction it said that a wolf came along and howled and took off in the

01:27:07.040 --> 01:27:13.870
direction of Fort Sill. It said that an eagle flew overhead and began flying in

01:27:13.870 --> 01:27:21.500
the direction of Fort Sill. Quanah took those signs to finally go to Fort Sill

01:27:21.500 --> 01:27:23.490
with the other Quahadas.

01:27:30.350 --> 01:27:34.800
With the Indians of the southern plains confined to reservations,

01:27:34.800 --> 01:27:40.880
the hide hunters, 3,000 of them by one estimate, went back to work.

01:27:40.880 --> 01:27:48.810
They considered 1876 a banner year for buffalo in the Texas panhandle.

01:27:48.810 --> 01:27:52.420
John Cook, who had left Kansas to join the hunt,

01:27:52.420 --> 01:27:59.640
killed 88 buffalo in one stand, alternating between two rifles when one overheated.

01:28:01.950 --> 01:28:07.510
A slight feeling of remorse would come over me for the part I was taking in this greatest

01:28:07.510 --> 01:28:11.030
of all hunts to the death.

01:28:11.030 --> 01:28:16.010
As I walked through where the carcasses lay the thickest, he later recounted, I could not

01:28:16.010 --> 01:28:23.880
help but think that I had done wrong to make such a slaughter for the hides alone.

01:28:23.880 --> 01:28:29.640
Then I would justify myself and pictured a white schoolhouse on that knoll yonder.

01:28:30.000 --> 01:28:34.560
a maid was teaching future generals and statesmen the necessity of becoming

01:28:34.560 --> 01:28:41.890
familiar with the three R's. Back on that plateau I could see a courthouse of a

01:28:41.890 --> 01:28:48.120
thriving county seat. Some of these days we will hear the whistle and shriek of a

01:28:48.120 --> 01:28:53.300
locomotive as she comes through the gap and not long until we can hear the

01:28:53.300 --> 01:29:00.000
lowing of cattle and the bleeding sheep and the mourning crow of the barnyard rooster.

01:29:02.400 --> 01:29:06.920
- Frank Mayer was less sentimental about it all.

01:29:06.920 --> 01:29:09.320
- Maybe we runners served our purpose

01:29:09.320 --> 01:29:12.230
in helping abolish the buffalo.

01:29:12.230 --> 01:29:14.910
Maybe it was our ruthless harvesting of him

01:29:14.910 --> 01:29:17.350
which telescoped the control of the Indian

01:29:17.350 --> 01:29:20.920
by a decade or maybe more.

01:29:20.920 --> 01:29:25.370
Or maybe I'm just rationalizing.

01:29:25.370 --> 01:29:27.940
Maybe we were just a greedy lot

01:29:27.940 --> 01:29:30.000
who wanted to get ours and to head.

01:29:30.000 --> 01:29:33.970
with posterity, the buffalo and anyone else,

01:29:34.050 --> 01:29:38.480
just so we kept our scalps on and our money pouches filled.

01:29:38.480 --> 01:29:42.310
I think maybe that's the way it was.

01:29:42.320 --> 01:29:49.200
By 1877, only three years after the fight at Adobe Walls,

01:29:49.200 --> 01:29:51.830
the immense herd south of the Arkansas

01:29:51.890 --> 01:29:55.060
had been reduced to a few scattered groups.

01:29:55.060 --> 01:29:59.280
By 1878, even those were disappearing.

01:30:00.320 --> 01:30:07.300
ranches, homesteads, and small towns were starting to fill what had been the buffalo's domain.

01:30:07.300 --> 01:30:13.160
For every Indian in the West, there were now 40 whites.

01:30:13.160 --> 01:30:18.430
The hide hunters' trading posts in Texas began closing.

01:30:18.430 --> 01:30:22.510
Dodge City was turning into a raucous cow town,

01:30:22.510 --> 01:30:28.520
where live cattle, not buffalo hides, were being loaded onto the railroad cars.

01:30:31.210 --> 01:30:40.120
we had killed the golden goose. Presently all I saw was rotting red carcasses or bleaching

01:30:40.120 --> 01:30:47.800
white bones, and the stench was so great that at a mile away from a stand you could smell

01:30:47.800 --> 01:31:00.000
it and be forced to hold your nose. Only the coyotes and wolves didn't seem to mind. To

01:31:00.000 --> 01:31:03.210
After his reservation's paltry food supply,

01:31:03.210 --> 01:31:10.940
Quanah got permission from the army to lead 300 Comanches and Kiowas on a buffalo hunt.

01:31:10.940 --> 01:31:17.790
They moved south, across familiar territory that now seemed an alien landscape, littered

01:31:17.790 --> 01:31:19.170
with bison carcasses.

01:31:19.170 --> 01:31:28.090
You know, it's in such a short span of time where the buffalo are plentiful, where that

01:31:28.090 --> 01:31:30.000
way of life is going so strong.

01:31:31.630 --> 01:31:43.350
And I can only imagine scenes of carnage,

01:31:43.350 --> 01:31:56.830
the rotting smells, while en route to search for buffalo.

01:31:56.830 --> 01:32:00.000
And so on our lands,

01:32:00.440 --> 01:32:07.790
are all these visual reminders of what others had done to us and to a way of life.

01:32:07.790 --> 01:32:16.430
Life was over in a sense, you know, to see such a thing as to see the death of a god.

01:32:16.430 --> 01:32:22.190
In disbelief, Quanah's group pushed on to Palo Duro Canyon,

01:32:22.190 --> 01:32:25.950
which had always been a reliable refuge for the bison.

01:32:25.950 --> 01:32:29.570
Instead of buffalo, they found a herd of cattle.

01:32:30.540 --> 01:32:34.670
The rancher who owned them rode out to Parley.

01:32:34.670 --> 01:32:39.800
Charles Goodnight had fought against Indians as a Texas ranger, and after the Battle of

01:32:39.800 --> 01:32:45.460
Palo Duro Canyon, he established the first cattle ranch there.

01:32:45.460 --> 01:32:50.880
Goodnight actually rode out to meet them when he saw them coming.

01:32:50.880 --> 01:32:55.540
He knew that Comanches respected bravery.

01:32:55.540 --> 01:33:00.000
They respected that kind of strength, and he rode out to meet them to

01:33:00.000 --> 01:33:04.670
could hopefully avoid any certain violent conflict.

01:33:04.670 --> 01:33:08.830
He said, "I'm Charles Goodnight and I just moved my ranch down here from Colorado."

01:33:08.830 --> 01:33:15.970
He didn't want to say he was a Texan because the Comanches and Texans were mortal enemies.

01:33:15.970 --> 01:33:22.930
So Goodnight and Quanah start talking with each other and they eventually set up something

01:33:22.930 --> 01:33:24.960
of their own treaty.

01:33:24.960 --> 01:33:30.000
Goodnight told them there were no longer any buffalo in the canyon, but they could continue

01:33:30.000 --> 01:33:37.280
hunt to see for themselves that it was true in the meantime if they stayed peaceful goodnight

01:33:37.280 --> 01:33:43.180
said kwanta's party could kill two of his cows every other day so they had something to eat

01:33:43.180 --> 01:33:50.650
so kwanta went up to look for the buffalo and there was none and and then he realized that

01:33:50.650 --> 01:34:00.000
his way of life and what they depended on was no more back on their reservation

01:34:00.000 --> 01:34:02.000
near the Wichita Mountains.

01:34:02.000 --> 01:34:06.120
The Kiowas recorded the summer of 1879

01:34:06.120 --> 01:34:08.230
as the horse-eating time.

01:34:08.230 --> 01:34:16.100
The Buffaloes saw that their day was over.

01:34:16.100 --> 01:34:20.080
They could protect their people no longer.

01:34:20.080 --> 01:34:24.410
The Kiowas were camped on the north side of Mount Scott.

01:34:24.410 --> 01:34:29.280
One young woman got up very early in the morning.

01:34:30.000 --> 01:34:37.110
The dawn mist was still rising from Medicine Creek, and as she looked across the water,

01:34:37.110 --> 01:34:47.540
peering through the haze, she saw the last buffalo herd appear like a spirit dream.

01:34:47.540 --> 01:34:52.000
Straight to Mount Scott, the leader of the herd, walked.

01:34:52.000 --> 01:34:58.450
Behind him came the cows and their calves, and the few young males who had survived.

01:35:00.000 --> 01:35:11.400
the woman watched the face of the mountain opened inside mount scott the world was green and fresh

01:35:11.910 --> 01:35:22.410
as it had been when she was a small girl the rivers ran clear not red into this world of beauty

01:35:23.050 --> 01:35:30.000
the buffalo walked never to be seen again old lady horse

01:35:33.040 --> 01:35:42.730
old lady horse. I want to cry when I think of her. I see what she saw a farewell of tragic

01:35:42.730 --> 01:35:54.700
significance. It's a shadow within a shadow. This dark mass of animal vitality moving inexorably

01:35:54.700 --> 01:36:00.000
away from existence. And it has for every native

01:36:00.000 --> 01:36:16.570
American man woman and child a significance that probably is ineffable to

01:36:16.570 --> 01:36:25.410
hunt in this place I want you to turn back from here if you don't I will fight

01:36:25.410 --> 01:36:30.000
you I will remain what I am until I die

01:36:30.000 --> 01:36:36.860
a hunter and when there are no buffalo or other game I will send my children to

01:36:36.860 --> 01:36:43.290
hunt and live on prairie mice for where an Indian is shot up in one place his

01:36:43.290 --> 01:36:51.940
body becomes weak sitting bull on the northern plains where the railroad had

01:36:51.940 --> 01:37:00.000
not yet arrived the buffalo were still plentiful the worst sentence the

01:37:00.000 --> 01:37:06.050
can ever be written about native people is and then gold was discovered on their

01:37:06.050 --> 01:37:11.560
land. It happened in California. It happened in Georgia with the Cherokee.

01:37:11.560 --> 01:37:19.160
It happened in Montana. It happened in the Black Hills. The Custer expedition

01:37:19.160 --> 01:37:27.450
discovers gold and the gold rush to the Black Hills is on. The Lakota are enraged

01:37:27.450 --> 01:37:30.000
because once again this is a direct

01:37:30.000 --> 01:37:36.500
violation of an explicit treaty provision. The U.S. government simply takes the

01:37:36.500 --> 01:37:42.320
Black Hills, orders the tribes onto a smaller reservation, and deems all of the

01:37:42.320 --> 01:37:47.120
tribes that are not compliant with that new edict to be enemies of the U.S.

01:37:47.120 --> 01:37:54.420
government. Large bands of the Lakota had refused to stay on their reservation and went

01:37:54.420 --> 01:38:00.000
to hunt in the rich buffalo ranges of Wyoming's Powder River and the eastern plains of

01:38:00.000 --> 01:38:08.080
Montana. They included the Honkpapas, led by a chief whose name, Tatanka Iyutaka,

01:38:08.080 --> 01:38:14.680
describes an intractable buffalo, resolute in the face of his enemies, sitting

01:38:14.680 --> 01:38:22.510
bull. The Lakotas attacked the survey crews and military escorts, working to extend the

01:38:22.510 --> 01:38:30.000
Northern Pacific Railway westward into the heart of their hunting grounds. A military camp

01:38:30.000 --> 01:38:36.310
pain to drive them back to the reservation had resulted in disaster, when George Armstrong

01:38:36.310 --> 01:38:43.290
Custer and more than 200 members of his 7th Cavalry were annihilated on the Little Bighorn

01:38:43.290 --> 01:38:50.070
by Sitting Bull and his allies in 1876.

01:38:50.070 --> 01:38:55.030
The Army's response was the same as it had been in the Southern Plains, a relentless

01:38:55.030 --> 01:39:00.000
pursuit that, within a year, forced the surrender of one band after another.

01:39:00.000 --> 01:39:07.410
another. But Sitting Bull and his people had escaped across the border into Canada, beyond

01:39:07.410 --> 01:39:15.480
the reach of American troops, where he intended to continue living off the buffalo. By 1880,

01:39:15.930 --> 01:39:26.950
the Canadian herd was gone too. Sitting Bull's people began to starve. In 1881, he led his 167

01:39:26.950 --> 01:39:30.000
followers south. Across the border,

01:39:30.320 --> 01:39:36.360
surrendered at the Standing Rock reservation near the spot where he was

01:39:36.360 --> 01:39:45.000
born sitting bull composed his own song a warrior I have been he sang now it is

01:39:45.000 --> 01:39:54.130
all over a hard time I have that same year the northern Pacific reached miles

01:39:54.130 --> 01:40:00.000
city in Montana territory soon 5,000 hide hunters and skis

01:40:00.000 --> 01:40:02.500
were spilling over the plains,

01:40:02.540 --> 01:40:05.420
from the Yellowstone River to the upper Missouri,

01:40:05.460 --> 01:40:08.710
where they set up what one army lieutenant called

01:40:08.710 --> 01:40:12.500
"a cordon of camps," blocking the Great Ranges

01:40:12.540 --> 01:40:14.350
and rendering it impossible

01:40:14.390 --> 01:40:17.430
for scarcely a single bison to escape.

01:40:17.470 --> 01:40:22.690
The killing commenced all over again.

01:40:22.730 --> 01:40:27.250
Meanwhile, in New York,

01:40:27.290 --> 01:40:30.000
31-year-old George Byrd Grinnell

01:40:30.000 --> 01:40:33.140
had become editor of Forest and Stream,

01:40:33.140 --> 01:40:35.920
a publication for hunters and fishermen

01:40:35.920 --> 01:40:39.570
that he was prodding to take on issues of conservation

01:40:39.570 --> 01:40:41.010
with more urgency.

01:40:41.010 --> 01:40:45.300
During the hide hunting on the Southern Plains,

01:40:45.300 --> 01:40:47.860
he had advocated for policies he called

01:40:47.860 --> 01:40:51.120
"just" and "honest" toward Native Americans

01:40:51.120 --> 01:40:52.530
that would, he wrote,

01:40:52.530 --> 01:40:56.390
"conscientiously aid in the increase of the buffalo

01:40:56.390 --> 01:41:00.000
instead of furthering its foolish and reckless slaughter."

01:41:01.560 --> 01:41:07.790
Now Grinnell turned his attention to what was unfolding in Montana.

01:41:07.790 --> 01:41:15.010
Up to within a few years ago, the valley of the Yellowstone River has been a magnificent hunting ground.

01:41:15.010 --> 01:41:20.850
The progress of the Northern Pacific Railroad, however, has changed all this.

01:41:20.850 --> 01:41:29.030
The buffalo will disappear unless steps are taken to protect it there.

01:41:31.180 --> 01:41:36.240
This is the era of the myth of inexhaustibility.

01:41:36.240 --> 01:41:40.140
The belief that the West is so vast,

01:41:40.140 --> 01:41:42.770
that the resources are so vast,

01:41:42.770 --> 01:41:46.020
that they can never be exhausted.

01:41:46.020 --> 01:41:49.650
But it was so much in front of them, what was happening,

01:41:49.650 --> 01:41:52.710
that I think they began to figure it out.

01:41:52.710 --> 01:41:55.980
It became more and more difficult to find buffalo.

01:41:55.980 --> 01:41:58.310
And there were ominous signs.

01:41:58.310 --> 01:42:00.000
Weird things began to happen.

01:42:00.030 --> 01:42:04.970
like they would find herds that were comprised entirely of calves.

01:42:04.970 --> 01:42:08.740
But there also was a capacity to deny

01:42:08.740 --> 01:42:12.700
and to believe that they had just gone over the next ridge line,

01:42:12.700 --> 01:42:16.090
gone into the next territory.

01:42:16.090 --> 01:42:19.480
And so all of that kind of mixes together.

01:42:19.480 --> 01:42:23.220
In Miles City in the fall of 1883,

01:42:23.220 --> 01:42:27.070
the hide hunters prepared for another winter on the plains,

01:42:27.070 --> 01:42:30.000
believing there must still be plenty of buffalo

01:42:30.090 --> 01:42:34.310
between the Yellowstone and Missouri Rivers.

01:42:34.310 --> 01:42:36.400
They came back in the spring

01:42:36.400 --> 01:42:40.340
with almost nothing to show for their efforts.

01:42:40.340 --> 01:42:41.940
- There are people in Miles City

01:42:41.940 --> 01:42:43.820
who had been hide hunters,

01:42:43.820 --> 01:42:45.640
and they're simply lolling around

01:42:45.640 --> 01:42:47.930
waiting for the return of the herds.

01:42:47.930 --> 01:42:50.590
They still thought there has to be some somewhere.

01:42:50.590 --> 01:42:54.330
When they'd finished, they didn't know they'd finished.

01:42:54.330 --> 01:43:00.000
They felt that, well, it can't be over, and it was over.

01:43:00.000 --> 01:43:10.370
In 1984, the total number of hides brought to the northern Pacific fit in a single boxcar.

01:43:10.370 --> 01:43:18.610
One by one, we runners put up our buffalo rifles, sold them, gave them away, or kept

01:43:18.610 --> 01:43:23.180
them for other hunting, and left the ranges.

01:43:23.180 --> 01:43:29.920
And there settled over them, a vast quiet, the buffalo was gone.

01:43:31.400 --> 01:43:43.510
frank mayer there is no no story anywhere in world history that involves as large a destruction of

01:43:43.510 --> 01:43:51.000
wild animals as happened in north america in the western united states in particular between 1800

01:43:51.000 --> 01:43:59.190
and about 1890 i mean it is the largest destruction of animal life discoverable in modern world history

01:44:00.250 --> 01:44:06.240
Why Americans are so destructive, I think, is an important question to ask.

01:44:06.240 --> 01:44:13.530
Why is that part of our story? Why is that part of our history?

01:44:13.530 --> 01:44:15.490
When the hide hunters went broke,

01:44:15.490 --> 01:44:18.550
some turned to killing other animals for the market,

01:44:18.550 --> 01:44:24.010
like antelope, elk, and grizzly bears.

01:44:24.010 --> 01:44:27.020
With wolf pelts worth $2 each in New York City,

01:44:27.020 --> 01:44:30.000
some hunters began lacing bison carcasses

01:44:30.000 --> 01:44:44.240
with strychnine, which poisoned not only wolves, but other scavengers, coyotes, foxes, bobcats, skunks, vultures, ravens, eagles.

01:44:44.240 --> 01:45:00.000
Other buffalo hunters left to pursue other work. Native people had no choice. They had to stay. And without

01:45:00.000 --> 01:45:06.770
buffalo meat to supplement their meager government rations, many starved.

01:45:06.770 --> 01:45:13.330
On the Blackfeet Reservation, an inspector checked on 23 lodges in one village.

01:45:13.330 --> 01:45:19.830
He reported seeing a rabbit being cooked in one, and a steer hoof in another.

01:45:19.830 --> 01:45:25.140
The other 21 lodges had no food at all.

01:45:25.140 --> 01:45:30.000
600 Blackfeet, a quarter of the tribe, perished during the

01:45:30.000 --> 01:45:36.450
winter of famine but that's really what the government wanted was for Indian

01:45:36.450 --> 01:45:41.450
people left to turn to the government and you had to take away all of the

01:45:41.450 --> 01:45:50.050
resources for that to happen it was devastating and it was heartbreaking we

01:45:50.050 --> 01:46:00.000
had the songs but no Buffalo to sing them to it's like a spiritual trauma nobody believed

01:46:00.000 --> 01:46:06.940
even then that the white man could kill all the buffalo even when he did not

01:46:06.940 --> 01:46:15.820
want the meat. Not believing their own eyes our hunters rode very far looking

01:46:15.820 --> 01:46:21.940
for buffalo so far away that even if they found a herd we could not have

01:46:21.940 --> 01:46:30.000
reached it in half a moon. Nothing. We found nothing they told us

01:46:30.700 --> 01:46:35.050
And then, hungry, they stared at the empty plains.

01:46:37.320 --> 01:46:41.280
It's so dreaming, pretty shilled.

01:46:41.280 --> 01:46:51.080
A cold wind blew across the prairie when the last buffalo fell, Sitting Bull said.

01:46:51.870 --> 01:46:53.750
A death wind for my people.

01:46:55.420 --> 01:46:57.040
It was devastating for us.

01:46:57.570 --> 01:46:59.390
That would have been the most heartbreaking thing.

01:47:00.670 --> 01:47:09.920
i couldn't imagine it i couldn't imagine the people what they were what they went through

01:47:09.920 --> 01:47:16.990
especially a father saying i gotta i gotta take care of my children i gotta take care of my clan

01:47:16.990 --> 01:47:26.400
i gotta take care of my society and i can't do it now a new buffalo business sprang up

01:47:26.400 --> 01:47:30.000
millions of buffalo skulls and bones were bleaching on

01:47:30.000 --> 01:47:37.030
under the prairie sun, and it turned out there was money to be made from them too.

01:47:37.030 --> 01:47:43.300
Companies in the East offered an average of $8 a ton for bones they could grind into fertilizer

01:47:43.300 --> 01:47:46.840
or use in refining sugar.

01:47:46.840 --> 01:47:54.550
Buffalo horns were turned into buttons, combs, and knife handles, hooves became glue.

01:47:54.550 --> 01:48:00.000
Homesteaders in Nebraska and Kansas, desperate for cash because drought was withering

01:48:00.030 --> 01:48:08.120
their crops turned to harvesting the skulls and skeletons still littering the plains.

01:48:08.120 --> 01:48:12.980
One entrepreneur in Texas stacked mounds of bones along the tracks of the Fort Worth and

01:48:12.980 --> 01:48:22.110
Denver Railroad and made $25,000. Buffalo Bones, a Kansas newspaper reported, are now

01:48:22.110 --> 01:48:30.000
legal tender in Dodge City. A company in St. Louis processed more than one million tons

01:48:30.000 --> 01:48:32.720
of bison bones.

01:48:32.720 --> 01:48:38.740
The Michigan Carbon Works became Detroit's largest industry.

01:48:38.740 --> 01:48:45.370
In the end, the bone trade would generate more profits for the bone pickers, the railroads,

01:48:45.370 --> 01:48:50.430
and the industries than the buffalo hides ever had.

01:48:50.430 --> 01:48:55.520
Even what remained of them was being taken away from their native ground.

01:48:55.520 --> 01:48:59.540
It was like grave robbing in a way.

01:49:00.000 --> 01:49:10.430
just strikes me as as a society trying to clean up a you know crime scene this is the murder of

01:49:10.430 --> 01:49:16.950
buffalo our brothers and let's get rid of that let's hide it let's get not only the buffalo out

01:49:16.950 --> 01:49:24.380
let's get the bones out too so they took everything from us and we understood that

01:49:24.380 --> 01:49:30.000
as a way of killing us off they're taking away our groceries

01:49:30.000 --> 01:49:36.010
store. And that's what they did. The buffalo was our grocery store. They killed the spirit

01:49:36.010 --> 01:49:41.680
of the buffalo in some cases, we thought. But that's why our prayers got stronger. That's

01:49:41.680 --> 01:49:46.020
why our people got stronger. They had to. If they didn't, we would have been killed

01:49:46.020 --> 01:49:52.820
off like the buffalo.

01:49:52.820 --> 01:50:00.000
By 1885, a species once numbering in the tens of millions had been reduced to fewer than

01:50:00.000 --> 01:50:06.380
a thousand, mostly small groups of a dozen or less, scattered in different

01:50:06.380 --> 01:50:12.460
corners across the West. Even those survivors were under assault from any

01:50:12.460 --> 01:50:18.130
hunters who could find them, looking now for trophy heads to hang on someone's

01:50:18.130 --> 01:50:22.920
wall. The American Buffalo was on the brink of

01:50:22.920 --> 01:50:30.000
extinction. But in a handful of places, people had begun trying

01:50:30.000 --> 01:50:36.880
to rescue a few bison and start small private herds.

01:50:36.880 --> 01:50:42.430
In the Texas panhandle, Charles Goodnight, at the urging of his wife Molly, had brought

01:50:42.430 --> 01:50:47.370
home two buffalo calves, which she was nurturing.

01:50:47.370 --> 01:50:53.900
In northwestern Montana, a young Pandorae Indian named La Tati had herded six calves

01:50:53.900 --> 01:50:59.160
from the Great Plains over the Rocky Mountains to the Flathead Reservation.

01:51:00.510 --> 01:51:06.640
In South Dakota, Fred Dupuis and Good Elk Woman, a French-Canadian rancher and his Lakota

01:51:06.640 --> 01:51:14.990
wife, had saved five calves from slaughter, bringing them home in a buckboard.

01:51:14.990 --> 01:51:21.490
And in a remote corner of the recently created Yellowstone National Park, the last free-roaming

01:51:21.490 --> 01:51:28.890
bison herd in the United States had found the semblance of sanctuary, though even there,

01:51:28.890 --> 01:51:30.000
poachers were beginning to win.

01:51:30.030 --> 01:51:38.070
whittle their numbers down. At the same time, George Bird Grinnell had begun a

01:51:38.070 --> 01:51:43.610
campaign to provide the park and its buffalo the protection they both needed

01:51:43.610 --> 01:51:52.110
for their survival. "We have seen the Indian and the game retreat before the

01:51:52.110 --> 01:51:57.970
white man and the cattle and beheld the tide of settlement move forward, which

01:51:57.970 --> 01:51:59.640
threatens before long.

01:52:00.000 --> 01:52:10.990
leave no portion of our vast territory unbroken by the farmer's plow or untrodden by his flocks.

01:52:10.990 --> 01:52:18.780
There is one spot left, a single rock, about which this tide will break and past which

01:52:18.780 --> 01:52:30.000
it will sweep, leaving it undefiled by the unsightly traces of civilization. Here, in

01:52:30.000 --> 01:52:37.550
Park. The large game of the West may be preserved from extermination. In this,

01:52:37.550 --> 01:52:43.960
their last refuge, George Bird Grinnell.

01:54:03.100 --> 01:54:05.600
Next time, on The American Buffalo...

01:54:05.600 --> 01:54:09.380
You don't get a lot of chances to correct history's mistakes.

01:54:09.380 --> 01:54:11.680
Unlikely alliances are formed.

01:54:11.680 --> 01:54:17.660
The early conservation movement is full of people who did the right thing for the wrong reasons.

01:54:17.660 --> 01:54:19.650
But is it already too late?

01:54:19.650 --> 01:54:21.650
This is The Buffalo's last chance.

01:54:21.650 --> 01:54:23.970
This is not just a story of tragedy.

01:54:23.970 --> 01:54:28.610
This is a story of persevering and continuing on.

01:54:28.610 --> 01:54:30.000
